"""Authored Class 11 lessons. Text is original; NCERT alignment is in the catalogue.
Each section supplies an introduction, reasoning, formula with units and limits,
a solved example, misconception, and a distinct retrieval question.
"""
CHAPTERS = []
def chapter(key, title, volume, pages, intro, ncert=None):
    c = dict(key=key, title=title, volume=volume, module_pages=pages, intro=intro, ncert=ncert, sections=[])
    CHAPTERS.append(c)
    return c

def section(c, slug, title, intro, reasoning, formula, symbols, trap, example, solution, question, options, answer, explanation):
    c['sections'].append(dict(id=c['key']+'-'+slug, title=title, intro=intro, reasoning=reasoning,
        formula=formula, symbols=symbols, trap=trap, example=example, solution=solution,
        question=question, options=options.split('|'), answer=answer, explanation=explanation))

c=chapter('maths','Mathematical Review & Physical World',1,'1–21',
'Build the mathematical language used throughout physics: relationships, graphs, rates and accumulation. Physical World is retained as a foundation from your coaching index; the index is not itself an official NEET syllabus.')
section(c,'models','Models, laws and sensible approximations',
'Physics describes observations with quantities that can be measured. A law summarises a reproducible relationship; a model keeps the features needed for a particular question. A particle model ignores the size of an object when its dimensions do not affect the motion being studied.',
'The same ball can be a particle in projectile motion and an extended body when its spin matters. State the system, reference frame and assumptions before choosing an equation. After calculating, check units, limiting cases and whether the magnitude is physically sensible.',
r'\text{prediction}=\text{model}(\text{measured inputs})',
'Prediction means the observable being calculated; model means a stated physical relationship; measured inputs carry units and uncertainty. This is a workflow, not an algebraic force law.',
'A more detailed model is useful only when the neglected effect matters to the question.',
'A ball travels 30 m while its diameter is 0.06 m. Is a particle model reasonable for its trajectory?',
'The diameter is 0.002 times the travel distance. If rotation and air drag are neglected, tracking the centre is reasonable. For rolling down a slope, rotational energy must also be included.',
'Which task requires treating a wheel as an extended body?',
'Finding its centre’s displacement|Calculating its rotational kinetic energy|Converting its speed to SI|Timing a straight journey',1,
'Rotational energy depends on the mass distribution through the moment of inertia. Centre motion, unit conversion and elapsed time do not require that distribution.')
section(c,'ratios','Proportionality, powers and scaling',
'A proportionality states how one quantity changes when the others are fixed. If a quantity depends on a square, doubling the input multiplies the output by four. Inverse dependence means the output decreases when that input increases.',
'Write the complete dependence before taking a ratio: constants cancel only if they really remain unchanged. This is the basis of many NEET comparison questions in gravitation, elasticity, heat and oscillations.',
r'Q=Cx^a y^b,\qquad \frac{Q_2}{Q_1}=\left(\frac{x_2}{x_1}\right)^a\left(\frac{y_2}{y_1}\right)^b',
'Q is the dependent quantity; C is a constant with the required units; x and y are inputs; a and b are dimensionless exponents. Subscripts 1 and 2 denote two cases with the same C.',
'An increase of 100% means doubling; it does not mean multiplication by 100.',
'For a fixed spring constant, a spring period varies as the square root of mass. What happens if mass becomes nine times larger?',
r'Using \(T\propto\sqrt m\), the period ratio is \(\sqrt9=3\). The mass increases ninefold but the period only triples.',
'A quantity obeys Q ∝ r²/L. If r doubles and L triples, Q becomes',
'2/3 of Q|4/3 of Q|6Q|12Q',1,
'The radius contributes a factor of four and the length contributes one-third, so the total factor is 4/3. Adding the changes would ignore the multiplicative relationship.')
section(c,'trig','Trigonometry and small angles',
'Sine, cosine and tangent connect a direction with perpendicular components. Draw the right triangle and identify the side opposite the chosen angle. Changing which axis the angle is measured from exchanges sine and cosine.',
'Angles in calculus and small-angle approximations must be in radians. The approximation sin θ ≈ θ comes from the near-straight behaviour of the sine curve at zero; it is useful for pendulums but not for large swings.',
r'\sin\theta=\frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{hypotenuse}},\quad \cos\theta=\frac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{hypotenuse}},\quad \theta_{\rm rad}=\theta_{\rm deg}\frac{\pi}{180}',
'θ is the angle; triangle lengths must use the same unit; trigonometric ratios are dimensionless. For |θ| ≪ 1 rad, sin θ ≈ tan θ ≈ θ and cos θ ≈ 1−θ²/2.',
'Do not substitute a degree value directly for θ in sin θ ≈ θ.',
'An incline rises 3 m for every 4 m horizontally. Find sin θ and cos θ.',
'The sloping length is 5 m by Pythagoras. Thus sin θ = 3/5 and cos θ = 4/5. The slope angle is measured from the horizontal.',
'At a small angle of 0.04 rad, sin θ is approximately',
'0.04|4|0.0008|1',0,
'In radians, the small-angle sine is approximately the angle itself. The correction starts at order θ³; 1 is instead the approximate cosine.')
section(c,'graphs','Graphs: slope and intercept',
'A graph is an equation made visible. Its horizontal and vertical axes must have quantities and units. A straight line has a constant rate of change; its intercept tells you the value of the vertical quantity when the horizontal quantity is zero.',
'An upward line means a positive slope, not necessarily a positive value of the plotted quantity. Always read what is on each axis: the slope of displacement versus time is velocity, while the slope of velocity versus time is acceleration.',
r'y=mx+b,\qquad m=\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}',
'x and y are the plotted quantities; m is slope with units [y]/[x]; b is the y-intercept with the same units as y; Δ denotes a difference. Use distinct points on a straight line.',
'The drawn steepness depends on axis scales; the numerical slope must be calculated from labelled values.',
'A velocity graph passes through (2 s, 5 m/s) and (6 s, 13 m/s). Find acceleration.',
'The velocity change is 8 m/s in 4 s, so acceleration is 2 m/s². Extrapolating gives an initial velocity of 1 m/s.',
'The slope of a force-versus-extension graph has units',
'N m|N/m|m/N|J/s',1,
'Slope divides the vertical force by the horizontal extension, giving N/m. It is the spring constant for a Hookean spring; multiplying gives work units instead.')
section(c,'calculus','Rates: differentiation',
'An average rate compares a finite change with the interval over which it occurs. An instantaneous rate is the limit over a very short interval. On a curve, it is the slope of the tangent at that point.',
'Differentiating position gives velocity and differentiating velocity gives acceleration. Constants vanish because they do not change. For a power of time, the exponent becomes a multiplier and the power decreases by one.',
r'\frac{d}{dt}(Ct^n)=nCt^{n-1},\qquad v=\frac{dx}{dt},\qquad a=\frac{dv}{dt}',
'x is position (m), t is time (s), v is velocity (m/s), a is acceleration (m/s²); C has units making Ctⁿ a position or the stated quantity; n is a dimensionless power.',
'Instantaneous speed is |v|; differentiating position gives signed velocity, not always a positive speed.',
'A position is x = 2t³ − 3t + 5 in SI units. Find velocity at t = 2 s.',
'Differentiating gives v = 6t² − 3. At 2 s this is 24 − 3 = 21 m/s. The position offset 5 m contributes no velocity.',
'If x = 4t² + 7 in SI units, acceleration is',
'4 m/s²|8 m/s²|7 m/s²|8t m/s²',1,
'One derivative gives v = 8t; a second gives a = 8 m/s². The constant offset does not affect either derivative.')
section(c,'integrals','Accumulation: integration and area',
'Integration reverses differentiation and also measures accumulated change. If a rate varies, split the time interval into tiny pieces and add rate times duration. On a graph this becomes the signed area under the curve.',
'To obtain position from velocity, add the initial position after finding the displacement. Areas below the time axis are negative for displacement; take their magnitudes when calculating total distance.',
r'\int_{t_1}^{t_2}Ct^n\,dt=\frac{C}{n+1}(t_2^{n+1}-t_1^{n+1}),\qquad \Delta x=\int v\,dt',
'C and n describe the rate; n ≠ −1 for the displayed power rule; t₁ and t₂ are limits (s); v is velocity (m/s); Δx is displacement (m). A velocity–time area therefore has units m.',
'An integral gives a change between limits; it does not supply an unknown initial value.',
'Velocity grows uniformly from 2 to 8 m/s over 3 s. Find displacement.',
'The area is a trapezium: average velocity (2+8)/2 = 5 m/s times 3 s gives 15 m. Using just the final velocity would give an incorrect 24 m.',
'A velocity is −3 m/s for 2 s, then +3 m/s for 2 s. Displacement and distance are',
'0 m and 12 m|12 m and 0 m|6 m and 6 m|0 m and 0 m',0,
'The signed areas cancel, giving zero displacement. Their magnitudes add to 6+6=12 m, which is the distance travelled.')

c=chapter('units','Units and Measurements',1,'22–106','Learn how to report a measurement, check an equation and propagate uncertainty. Measurement instruments and error analysis connect the mathematics to practical experiments.',1)
section(c,'si','Physical quantities, SI units and conversion',
'A physical quantity combines a numerical value and a unit. The number changes when the unit changes, while the measured quantity does not. SI uses seven base quantities: length, mass, time, electric current, temperature, amount of substance and luminous intensity.',
'Derived units are combinations of base units. Convert each factor before substituting into a formula: cm² carries a squared conversion factor, and a cubic centimetre is a millionth of a cubic metre.',
r'1\,\mathrm{N}=1\,\mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-2}},\quad 1\,\mathrm{J}=1\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2\,s^{-2}},\quad 1\,\mathrm{km\,h^{-1}}=\frac5{18}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}',
'N is newton (force); J is joule (energy); kg, m and s are SI mass, length and time units. Kelvin, ampere, mole and candela are the other base units.',
'Convert areas and volumes with the square or cube of the length conversion, respectively.',
'A density is 2.7 g/cm³. Convert it to kg/m³.',
'One gram is 10⁻³ kg and one cm³ is 10⁻⁶ m³. Thus the density is 2.7×10³ = 2700 kg/m³.',
'72 km/h expressed in SI is',
'10 m/s|20 m/s|72 m/s|259.2 m/s',1,
'Multiply by 5/18: 72×5/18=20 m/s. Using 18/5 would perform the reverse conversion.')
section(c,'dimensions','Dimensions and dimensional consistency',
'Dimensions describe the type of a quantity rather than its chosen unit. Both metres and centimetres measure length and have dimension L. Every term added in a physical equation must have the same dimensions.',
'A dimensionally incorrect equation is impossible. A dimensionally correct one can still have a wrong numerical coefficient or omit physical effects. Angles, ratios of like quantities and arguments of logarithms are dimensionless.',
r'[F]=MLT^{-2},\quad [E]=ML^2T^{-2},\quad [G]=M^{-1}L^3T^{-2}',
'Square brackets denote dimensions; M, L and T represent mass, length and time; F is force, E is energy and G is the gravitational constant. Dimensional exponents are independent of the SI unit choice.',
'Dimensional analysis cannot distinguish a factor of 1/2 from 2 or determine a sine dependence.',
'Find the dimensions of pressure.',
r'Pressure is force/area, so \([p]=MLT^{-2}/L^2=ML^{-1}T^{-2}\). Its SI unit is Pa = N/m².',
'The coefficient k in F = kv² has dimensions',
'M/L|ML|M/T|ML/T²',0,
'k = F/v². Dividing MLT⁻² by L²T⁻² gives ML⁻¹. The coefficient is not a spring constant because its defining equation is different.')
section(c,'analysis','Constructing a dependence using dimensions',
'If a quantity depends on a few known variables, dimensional powers can constrain its form. Guess a product of powers, equate exponents of each base dimension, and solve the resulting equations.',
'This method works when the list of relevant variables is complete and there is no unresolved dimensionless dependence. The coefficient must be obtained from theory or experiment. For a pendulum, dimensions identify the length and gravity scaling but cannot give 2π.',
r'T_p=C\ell^a g^b\ \Longrightarrow\ a=\tfrac12,\ b=-\tfrac12,\quad T_p=C\sqrt{\ell/g}',
'Tₚ is pendulum period (s); ℓ is length (m); g is gravitational acceleration (m/s²); C is dimensionless; a and b are exponents. This analysis assumes no other relevant dimensional input.',
'A dimensionless input such as the swing angle can affect C; dimensional analysis alone cannot establish amplitude independence.',
'Suppose wave speed depends only on string tension F and linear density μ. Find the dependence.',
'Let v ∝ Fᵃμᵇ. Matching mass gives a+b=0; time gives −2a=−1, so a=1/2 and b=−1/2. Hence v ∝ √(F/μ).',
'If a time depends only on distance ℓ and acceleration a, its dimensional form is',
'Cℓa|C√(ℓ/a)|C√(a/ℓ)|Cℓ/a',1,
'ℓ/a has dimension T²; its square root is a time. C cannot be fixed from dimensions.')
section(c,'significant','Significant figures and rounding',
'Significant figures indicate the precision represented by a measured number. Leading zeros only locate the decimal point; zeros between nonzero digits are significant. Trailing zeros after a decimal point are significant.',
'For addition or subtraction, round to the least precise decimal place. For multiplication or division, round to the smallest number of significant figures among measured inputs. Keep extra digits during intermediate steps and round the final result once.',
r'0.00450=4.50\times10^{-3}\quad(3\ \text{significant figures})',
'4.50 is the measured coefficient and the power of ten fixes scale. Exact counts and defined conversion factors do not limit significant figures; measurement precision does.',
'Writing 1500 without context is ambiguous; scientific notation makes intended precision explicit.',
'Evaluate 12.11 m + 0.3 m with appropriate precision.',
'The unrounded sum is 12.41 m. The second measurement is only precise to tenths, so report 12.4 m, not 12.41 m.',
'How many significant figures are in 0.02040?',
'2|3|4|5',2,
'The leading zeros are placeholders. The digits 2, 0, 4 and the final decimal zero are significant, so there are four.')
section(c,'errors','Accuracy, precision and error estimates',
'Accuracy means closeness to the accepted value; precision means repeatability or narrow spread. Repeated measurements can reduce random scatter but do not automatically remove a calibration bias. A mean gives a useful best estimate when random errors dominate.',
'For repeated readings, the mean absolute deviation is one elementary error estimate. Instrument resolution and known systematic errors must also be considered. Report an absolute uncertainty in the same unit as the quantity; relative uncertainty is a ratio.',
r'\bar x=\frac1N\sum x_i,\quad \Delta x=\frac1N\sum|x_i-\bar x|,\quad \text{percentage uncertainty}=100\frac{\Delta x}{|\bar x|}',
'xᵢ are readings; N is their count; x̄ is the mean; Δx is the mean absolute deviation, in the unit of x. The percentage formula assumes a nonzero mean and is not a universal statistical confidence interval.',
'Closely clustered readings can all be wrong if the instrument has a zero error.',
'Readings are 9.8, 10.0 and 10.2 cm. Find mean and mean absolute deviation.',
'The mean is 10.0 cm. Deviations are 0.2, 0 and 0.2 cm; their average is 0.133… cm, approximately 0.13 cm before choosing reporting precision.',
'An instrument consistently reads 0.5 cm too high. Repeating the reading mainly fails to remove',
'Random fluctuations|Systematic error|Sample count|Unit conversion',1,
'A fixed calibration bias shifts every reading. Averaging can reduce independent random scatter, but the bias needs a correction.')
section(c,'propagation','Propagation of measurement uncertainty',
'A calculated quantity inherits uncertainties from its measured inputs. For a worst-case estimate, absolute uncertainties add in sums, while fractional uncertainties add with the absolute values of the powers in products and quotients.',
'These are first-order bounds for small uncertainties, not a claim that independent random errors always add linearly. A squared radius therefore contributes twice its fractional uncertainty to area; this makes radius measurements especially important.',
r'z=x\pm y:\ \Delta z\leq\Delta x+\Delta y;\qquad z=Cx^a y^b:\ \frac{\Delta z}{|z|}\lesssim |a|\frac{\Delta x}{|x|}+|b|\frac{\Delta y}{|y|}',
'x, y and z are measured/calculated quantities; Δ denotes an absolute uncertainty; C is exact here; a and b are powers. The product formula is a first-order worst-case estimate for small relative errors and nonzero x, y.',
'Uncertainties do not cancel merely because the measured quantities are subtracted.',
'For density m/(πr²ℓ), errors in m, r and ℓ are 1%, 2% and 1%. Estimate the maximum percentage error.',
'Add the weighted fractional uncertainties: 1+2×2+1=6%. π is an exact mathematical constant and adds no measurement uncertainty.',
'If the radius uncertainty is 3%, the approximate area uncertainty is',
'1.5%|3%|6%|9%',2,
'Area varies as r², so the first-order fractional error doubles to 6%. Squaring 3% is not the propagation rule.')
section(c,'instruments','Vernier calipers, screw gauge and zero correction',
'A fine scale subdivides what the main scale cannot resolve. For a direct vernier, the least count is the difference between one main-scale division and one vernier-scale division. A screw gauge converts rotation into a small linear motion.',
'Combine the main reading with the coinciding vernier or circular reading. Then subtract the zero error: an instrument that reads positive when it should read zero is reading too high. Check calibration before and after an experiment.',
r'LC_v=1\,MSD-1\,VSD,\quad LC_s=\frac{p}{N},\quad x_{\rm true}=x_{\rm observed}-e_0',
'LCᵥ is direct-vernier least count; MSD and VSD are scale division lengths; LCₛ is screw-gauge least count; p is pitch (advance per full turn); N is divisions per turn; e₀ is signed zero error. All lengths use consistent units.',
'Zero correction has the opposite sign to zero error. A negative zero error requires adding its magnitude.',
'A screw gauge has pitch 0.5 mm and 50 divisions. Observed diameter is 2.36 mm and zero error is +0.02 mm. Find corrected diameter.',
'The least count is 0.5/50=0.01 mm. Corrected diameter is 2.36−0.02=2.34 mm.',
'An observed length is 5.12 cm and zero error is −0.03 cm. The corrected length is',
'5.09 cm|5.12 cm|5.15 cm|5.03 cm',2,
'Subtract the signed error: 5.12−(−0.03)=5.15 cm. The negative zero error means the instrument underreads.')

c=chapter('linear','Motion in a Straight Line',1,'107–201','Separate position, displacement and distance; connect graphs with equations; then handle relative motion, gravity and changes of direction.',2)
section(c,'position','Position, displacement and distance',
'Choose an origin and a positive direction. Position is the signed coordinate relative to that origin; displacement is the change in coordinate. Distance counts every part of the path and is never negative.',
'A body can return to its starting point with zero displacement but a positive distance. Divide the path at each turning point to calculate distance. A coordinate can be positive while the body is moving in the negative direction.',
r'\Delta x=x_f-x_i,\qquad d=\sum_j|\Delta x_j|',
'xᵢ and x_f are initial and final positions (m); Δx is displacement (m); d is total distance (m); j labels path segments without reversal. Coordinates depend on the chosen origin.',
'Position sign, velocity sign and acceleration sign describe three different things.',
'A particle goes from x=−2 m to x=5 m, then returns to x=1 m. Find displacement and distance.',
'Displacement is 1−(−2)=3 m. Distance is 7+4=11 m. The return journey decreases position while increasing distance.',
'A descending lift above the ground slows before stopping. With upward positive and ground as origin, the signs of x, v, a are',
'+, +, +|+, −, +|−, −, −|+, −, −',1,
'The position is above the origin, velocity is downward, and acceleration is upward because the downward motion is slowing. None of these signs is inferred from another alone.')
section(c,'averages','Average and instantaneous velocity and speed',
'Average velocity uses net displacement over total elapsed time. Average speed uses total distance over that time. Instantaneous speed is the magnitude of instantaneous velocity.',
'For equal time intervals, speeds average arithmetically. For equal distances, the slower leg takes longer and has a greater effect: calculate total distance divided by total time rather than averaging the speeds.',
r'\bar v=\frac{\Delta x}{\Delta t},\quad \bar s=\frac{d}{\Delta t},\quad \bar s_{\rm equal\ distances}=\frac{2uv}{u+v}',
'v̄ is average signed velocity and s̄ is average speed (m/s); Δt is positive elapsed time (s); d is distance (m); u and v are positive speeds for two equal-distance legs. The last formula is not for equal-time legs.',
'An average of speeds is not generally the average speed of a journey.',
'A rider covers 120 m at 4 m/s and another 120 m at 6 m/s. Find average speed.',
'Times are 30 s and 20 s. Total distance 240 m divided by 50 s gives 4.8 m/s.',
'For equal times at 3 and 9 m/s in the same direction, average speed is',
'4.5 m/s|6 m/s|3 m/s|9 m/s',1,
'Each speed acts for the same time, so the average is (3+9)/2=6 m/s. The harmonic mean would apply to equal distances.')
section(c,'acceleration','Acceleration and whether speed increases',
'Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. Positive acceleration means velocity becomes more positive; it does not always mean speed increases. A negative velocity with positive acceleration moves toward zero and initially slows.',
'In one dimension, speed increases when velocity and acceleration have the same sign. At a reversal the velocity passes through zero; acceleration need not be zero. For nonuniform motion, differentiate the given position or velocity function.',
r'a=\frac{dv}{dt},\qquad \frac{d(v^2)}{dt}=2va',
'v is signed velocity (m/s); a is acceleration (m/s²); t is time (s). The sign of va determines whether speed squared increases or decreases; the speed derivative at v=0 needs separate care.',
'Negative acceleration is not a synonym for slowing down.',
'A car has v=−8 m/s and a=+2 m/s² for 2 s. What is its final velocity and speed change?',
'Final velocity is −8+2×2=−4 m/s. The speed falls from 8 to 4 m/s even though acceleration is positive.',
'An object has v=−5 m/s and a=−2 m/s². Its speed is',
'Increasing|Decreasing|Constant|Necessarily zero',0,
'Both signs are negative, so va is positive. The velocity becomes more negative and its magnitude increases.')
section(c,'equations','Constant-acceleration equations',
'When acceleration is constant, velocity changes linearly with time. The average of initial and final velocities then gives displacement. Eliminating time produces the equation that is useful when a stopping distance is known.',
'Choose a sign convention, list known quantities, and select the equation missing the unwanted unknown. These equations do not apply unchanged when acceleration depends on time, position or speed.',
r'v=u+at,\quad s=ut+\tfrac12at^2,\quad v^2=u^2+2as,\quad s=\tfrac12(u+v)t',
'u and v are initial/final velocities (m/s); a is constant acceleration (m/s²); t is elapsed time (s); s is signed displacement (m). All velocities, displacements and accelerations use the same chosen positive direction.',
'If motion reverses, s is still net displacement; distance requires splitting at the turning time.',
'A car moving at 20 m/s brakes with acceleration −4 m/s². Find stopping time and stopping distance.',
'From 0=20−4t, t=5 s. From 0=20²+2(−4)s, s=50 m. The signs give a positive distance in the initial direction.',
'Doubling the initial speed with the same constant braking magnitude changes stopping distance by a factor',
'1|2|4|8',2,
'Stopping distance is u²/(2|a|), so doubling u quadruples it. Stopping time only doubles.')
section(c,'graphs','Reading x–t, v–t and a–t graphs',
'An x–t slope gives velocity; a v–t slope gives acceleration. The signed area under v–t gives displacement, and area under a–t gives the velocity change. A flat x–t segment means rest, whereas a flat v–t segment means constant velocity.',
'For distance, add absolute areas under a velocity graph. A vertical jump in an x–t graph would mean an instantaneous change of position and is not an ordinary finite-speed motion. A kink represents an abrupt velocity change in an idealised model.',
r'v=\text{slope of }x(t),\quad a=\text{slope of }v(t),\quad \Delta x=\int v\,dt,\quad \Delta v=\int a\,dt',
'x is position (m), v velocity (m/s), a acceleration (m/s²), t time (s). Slopes and areas use labelled numerical axes, not the visual angle or pixel area.',
'An area under x–t does not represent displacement.',
'Velocity rises from 0 to 6 m/s in 2 s, stays at 6 for 3 s, then falls to 0 in 2 s. Find distance.',
'Add the two triangles and rectangle: ½×2×6 + 3×6 + ½×2×6 = 30 m. Velocity is nonnegative, so distance equals displacement.',
'A horizontal line above zero on a velocity–time graph represents',
'Rest|Constant positive velocity|Increasing acceleration|Increasing speed',1,
'Its height is a positive velocity and its slope is zero acceleration. Rest would require the line to lie on the time axis.')
section(c,'gravity','Vertical motion and free fall',
'Near Earth’s surface and without air resistance, every freely falling object has the same downward acceleration. An upward throw loses upward velocity until the highest point, then gains downward velocity. Gravity continues to act at the top.',
'Take upward as positive so a=−g. For return to the launch height, upward and downward travel times match. For a different final height, solve the displacement equation instead of using the symmetric-flight shortcut.',
r'v=u-gt,\quad y-y_0=ut-\tfrac12gt^2,\quad H=\frac{u^2}{2g},\quad T=\frac{2u}{g}',
'u is upward launch speed (m/s); v is signed vertical velocity (m/s); g is positive gravity magnitude (m/s²); y and y₀ are heights (m); H is maximum rise above launch; T is time back to launch height. g is assumed constant.',
'At the highest point velocity is zero but acceleration remains −g.',
'A ball is thrown vertically upward at 15 m/s. Use g=10 m/s². Find rise and return time.',
'Maximum rise is 225/20=11.25 m. Time to top is 1.5 s and back to the same height is 3 s.',
'Ignoring drag, the acceleration of a vertically thrown ball at its highest point is',
'Zero|g upward|g downward|Dependent on its mass',2,
'Gravity does not switch off when velocity vanishes. The mass cancels from F=ma for the gravitational force mg.')
section(c,'relative','Relative motion and meeting conditions',
'Relative position is one object’s coordinate measured from another. In a common inertial frame, subtract their positions, velocities and accelerations. Meeting occurs when their relative position becomes zero.',
'For opposite directions the speed of approach is the sum of speed magnitudes; for the same direction it is their difference. Accelerating objects require the relative displacement equation, not a constant closing-speed shortcut.',
r'x_{A/B}=x_A-x_B,\quad v_{A/B}=v_A-v_B,\quad a_{A/B}=a_A-a_B',
'A and B label objects; x is signed position (m), v velocity (m/s), a acceleration (m/s²); A/B means A as seen from B. Use one shared coordinate axis before subtracting.',
'Two free-falling objects have zero relative acceleration if they experience the same g, even when each accelerates relative to the ground.',
'Two runners are 100 m apart and run toward each other at 6 and 4 m/s. When do they meet?',
'The separation decreases at 10 m/s. Time is 100/10=10 s. Their respective distances are 60 m and 40 m.',
'Two vehicles move in the same direction at 18 and 12 m/s. Their relative speed is',
'30 m/s|6 m/s|18 m/s|12 m/s',1,
'Subtract same-direction velocities: 18−12=6 m/s. Adding is appropriate for opposing directions.')

c=chapter('vectors','Vectors',1,'202–243','Understand direction before using projectile, force or torque equations. Vectors are retained as their own module to match your uploaded sequence.',3)
section(c,'components','Scalars, vectors and components',
'A scalar has magnitude only; a vector also has direction and follows vector addition. Choose perpendicular axes and project a vector onto them. Components can be negative even when the magnitude is positive.',
'The horizontal component uses cosine if the angle is measured from the horizontal. Reconstruct the magnitude with Pythagoras and use the signs to choose the correct quadrant for its direction.',
r'\vec A=A_x\hat i+A_y\hat j,\quad A_x=A\cos\theta,\quad A_y=A\sin\theta,\quad A=\sqrt{A_x^2+A_y^2}',
'A is nonnegative magnitude; Aₓ and Aᵧ are signed components in the unit of A; î and ĵ are dimensionless unit vectors along x and y; θ is measured from +x. Orthogonal Cartesian axes are assumed.',
'The numerical magnitude of a sum is not generally the sum of the magnitudes.',
'A 10 N force acts 37° above horizontal. Use cos37°=0.8 and sin37°=0.6. Resolve it.',
'The components are Fₓ=8 N and Fᵧ=6 N. Their magnitude √(8²+6²)=10 N checks the result.',
'A vector has components −3 and +4 m. Its magnitude is',
'1 m|5 m|7 m|−5 m',1,
'The magnitude is √(9+16)=5 m. Negative x places the direction in quadrant II; it does not make the magnitude negative.')
section(c,'addition','Adding and subtracting vectors',
'Place arrows head to tail to add vectors; the resultant runs from the first tail to the last head. In components, add x values separately from y values. Subtracting a vector means adding an arrow with the opposite direction.',
'For two vectors with a known included angle, the cosine rule gives the resultant magnitude. The result lies between the difference and sum of the magnitudes, which provides a quick plausibility check.',
r'\vec R=\vec A+\vec B,\qquad R^2=A^2+B^2+2AB\cos\theta',
'R, A and B are magnitudes in the same unit; θ is the angle between A and B when drawn from a common tail; vector components add algebraically. For subtraction, replace B with −B.',
'Use the angle between the original vectors, not the supplementary angle inside an arbitrarily drawn triangle.',
'Two perpendicular forces of 6 N and 8 N act on a particle. Find the net force.',
'The dot term is zero because cos90°=0. Thus R=√(36+64)=10 N; its direction makes tan⁻¹(8/6) with the 6 N force.',
'Two 5 N vectors at 120° have a resultant magnitude',
'0 N|5 N|10 N|5√3 N',1,
'R²=25+25+50 cos120°=25, hence R=5 N. Adding the magnitudes would assume parallel forces.')
section(c,'dot','Dot product and projection',
'The dot product measures how much of one vector lies along another. It produces a scalar. Work is a dot product because only the force component along displacement transfers energy through that displacement.',
'Perpendicular vectors have zero dot product; oppositely directed vectors have a negative dot product. A projection can be signed, so an opposing force does negative work rather than zero work.',
r'\vec A\cdot\vec B=AB\cos\theta=A_xB_x+A_yB_y+A_zB_z',
'A and B are magnitudes; θ is their included angle; Cartesian component products sum to the dot product. Its unit is the product of the units of A and B; the projection of A along B is A cosθ for B ≠ 0.',
'A zero dot product means perpendicularity only when both vectors are nonzero.',
'Find the dot product of (2î−3ĵ) m and (4î+ĵ) m.',
'The result is 2×4+(−3)×1=5 m². There are no cross-component terms because î·ĵ=0.',
'The dot product of nonzero perpendicular vectors is',
'AB|−AB|0|A+B',2,
'cos90°=0. AB and −AB instead correspond to parallel and antiparallel directions.')
section(c,'cross','Cross product and the right-hand rule',
'The cross product produces a vector perpendicular to the plane of the two input vectors. Its magnitude measures the perpendicular combination; its direction follows the right-hand rule from the first vector toward the second.',
'Torque uses position crossed with force, so force along the position vector produces no torque. Reversing the order reverses the result. Use a labelled axis to say whether the perpendicular direction is into or out of the page.',
r'\vec A\times\vec B=AB\sin\theta\,\hat n,\qquad \hat i\times\hat j=\hat k,\quad \vec B\times\vec A=-\vec A\times\vec B',
'θ is the included angle, n̂ is the right-hand unit normal, î/ĵ/k̂ are x/y/z unit vectors; magnitude has the product unit of A and B. The cross product is zero for parallel nonzero vectors.',
'AB sinθ is only the magnitude; it does not state the direction of the cross product.',
'For position 2î m and force 3ĵ N, find torque.',
'τ = (2î)×(3ĵ)=6k̂ N m. It points along +z, out of an xy page drawn with +x right and +y up.',
'If A×B points along +z, B×A points',
'Along +z|Along −z|Along +x|Nowhere because it vanishes',1,
'The cross product is antisymmetric. Changing the order changes the sign, not the magnitude.')
section(c,'equilibrium','Resolving several vectors and equilibrium',
'A zero resultant means all vector components sum to zero. It does not mean each vector is zero. In a force balance, resolving forces along carefully chosen axes often reduces the problem to two simple equations.',
'A triangle of forces can close when the net force vanishes. For a rigid object, force balance alone is insufficient: torque must also vanish. For a particle, zero net force means zero acceleration, including motion at constant velocity.',
r'\sum\vec F=0\ \Longleftrightarrow\ \sum F_x=0,\ \sum F_y=0,\ \sum F_z=0',
'F is force (N); x, y and z denote orthogonal axes. The equivalence states translational equilibrium in an inertial frame; rigid-body rotational equilibrium additionally requires zero net torque.',
'An object in force equilibrium may move uniformly; it need not be at rest.',
'Forces (3î+4ĵ) N and (−î+2ĵ) N act. What third force balances them?',
'The first two sum to (2î+6ĵ) N. The third must be (−2î−6ĵ) N.',
'For forces 4î N and −3ĵ N, a balancing force is',
'4î−3ĵ N|−4î+3ĵ N|−4î−3ĵ N|3î−4ĵ N',1,
'Negate both components of the resultant. Balancing one component while leaving the other nonzero would still accelerate the object.')

c=chapter('plane','Motion in a Plane',1,'244–315','Resolve two-dimensional motion into perpendicular directions, then connect projectiles, relative velocity and circular motion.',3)
section(c,'kinematics','Two-dimensional position, velocity and acceleration',
'Position in a plane requires two coordinates. Velocity is the rate of change of the position vector and points tangent to the path. Acceleration can change speed, direction or both; it need not point along velocity.',
'With constant acceleration, apply the one-dimensional equations independently to x and y using the same time. After finding components, reconstruct magnitudes and directions with vector rules.',
r'\vec r=\vec r_0+\vec u t+\tfrac12\vec a t^2,\qquad \vec v=\vec u+\vec a t',
'r and r₀ are final/initial positions (m); u and v are velocities (m/s); a is constant acceleration (m/s²); t is elapsed time (s). Each vector equation holds component by component in an inertial frame.',
'Independence of perpendicular components does not imply equal accelerations or equal velocities.',
'A particle starts at the origin with u=(3î+4ĵ) m/s and a=2î m/s². Find its position after 2 s.',
'x=3×2+½×2×4=10 m and y=4×2=8 m. Thus r=(10î+8ĵ) m.',
'For v = 6î+8ĵ m/s, speed is',
'2 m/s|10 m/s|14 m/s|48 m/s',1,
'Speed is the magnitude √(6²+8²)=10 m/s, not the sum of perpendicular components.')
section(c,'projectiles','Projectile motion from component independence',
'Without drag, horizontal velocity stays constant while vertical velocity changes under gravity. At the top the vertical component vanishes, but the horizontal component remains. Combining x(t) and y(t) gives a parabola.',
'The standard range and flight-time formulas require launch and landing at the same height on horizontal ground. For a raised platform or a target at another height, solve the vertical equation for time and then use horizontal motion.',
r'x=u\cos\theta\,t,\quad y=u\sin\theta\,t-\tfrac12gt^2,\quad T=\frac{2u\sin\theta}{g},\quad H=\frac{u^2\sin^2\theta}{2g},\quad R=\frac{u^2\sin2\theta}{g}',
'u is launch speed (m/s); θ is launch angle above horizontal; x and y are displacement from launch (m); g is downward gravity magnitude (m/s²); T, H and R are same-level flight time (s), maximum rise (m) and range (m). Assume constant g and no drag.',
'45° maximises range only for a fixed speed and equal launch/landing heights.',
'A projectile launches at 20 m/s at 30°. Use g=10 m/s². Find flight time and maximum rise.',
'Vertical launch component is 10 m/s. T=2×10/10=2 s and H=10²/(2×10)=5 m.',
'At a projectile’s highest point, its acceleration is',
'Zero|Horizontal|Vertically downward|Along its velocity',2,
'Gravity remains downward throughout the flight. Velocity is horizontal at the top, so acceleration and velocity are perpendicular there.')
section(c,'horizontal','Horizontal launch and targets at another height',
'A horizontally launched projectile has zero initial vertical velocity. Its fall time is set by height and gravity; horizontal speed determines how far it travels during that time. Horizontal motion does not delay the vertical fall in the ideal model.',
'For an inclined launch from a platform, keep the initial vertical component in the equation and choose the positive root for the physical landing time. Measure the height relative to the target rather than switching origins midway.',
r't=\sqrt{\frac{2h}{g}},\qquad R=u_x\sqrt{\frac{2h}{g}},\qquad v_y=-gt',
'h is launch height above the landing plane (m); uₓ is horizontal launch speed (m/s); t is landing time (s); R is horizontal range (m); vᵧ is vertical velocity with upward positive. These formulas assume horizontal launch, constant g and no drag.',
'The final speed includes both horizontal and vertical components.',
'A ball leaves a 20 m high platform horizontally at 5 m/s. With g=10 m/s², find its range.',
'Time to fall is √(40/10)=2 s. Horizontal range is 5×2=10 m; vertical impact velocity is −20 m/s.',
'Two balls are released from the same height, one dropped and one launched horizontally. Neglecting drag, they reach the ground',
'At the same time|Dropped ball first|Launched ball first|According to their masses',0,
'Both have zero initial vertical velocity and the same vertical acceleration. Their horizontal velocities affect only horizontal travel.')
section(c,'relative','Relative velocity: rain and river crossings',
'In a plane, relative velocity is a vector difference. A swimmer’s velocity relative to water adds to the water’s velocity relative to ground. Rain appears tilted to a moving observer because the observer subtracts their own horizontal velocity.',
'The shortest crossing time occurs when all swimming speed is directed across the river; drifting is then allowed. To arrive directly opposite, aim upstream to cancel the current, which leaves a smaller across-river component.',
r'\vec v_{A/G}=\vec v_{A/B}+\vec v_{B/G},\quad t_{\min}=\frac{d}{u},\quad t_{\rm opposite}=\frac{d}{\sqrt{u^2-w^2}}',
'A is swimmer, B water, G ground; d is river width (m); u is swimmer speed relative to water (m/s); w is current speed (m/s). The directly-opposite formula requires u>w and an upstream component equal to w.',
'Shortest time and shortest ground path are different optimisation questions.',
'A swimmer has speed 5 m/s relative to water and the current is 3 m/s. For a 40 m river, find time to arrive directly opposite.',
'The swimmer uses 3 m/s upstream and √(25−9)=4 m/s across. Time is 40/4=10 s. A minimum-time crossing would take 8 s but drift.',
'For the same swimmer and river, the shortest crossing time is',
'8 s|10 s|13.3 s|20 s',0,
'Point entirely across to use the full 5 m/s across the 40 m width. Time is 8 s; current creates drift but does not change that crossing time.')
section(c,'circular','Uniform circular motion and centripetal acceleration',
'Uniform circular motion has constant speed but continuously changing velocity direction. The change in velocity points toward the centre in the small-time limit. This requires an inward acceleration even when the speed is constant.',
'Angular displacement measures rotation in radians. Speed equals radius times angular speed. The force providing inward acceleration may be tension, friction, gravity or a combination; centripetal force is a role of the net radial force, not an additional force.',
r'v=\omega r,\quad \omega=\frac{2\pi}{T},\quad a_c=\frac{v^2}{r}=\omega^2r',
'v is tangential speed (m/s); ω is angular speed (rad/s); r is radius (m); T is period (s); a_c is inward acceleration (m/s²). Uniform motion has no tangential acceleration.',
'A constant speed does not mean a constant velocity vector.',
'A particle moves at 6 m/s on a circle of radius 3 m. Find centripetal acceleration.',
'a_c=36/3=12 m/s² toward the centre. Its angular speed is 6/3=2 rad/s.',
'If speed doubles on the same circle, centripetal acceleration becomes',
'Half|Twice|Four times|Unchanged',2,
'a_c scales with v² at fixed radius. Doubling speed therefore gives a factor of four.')
section(c,'nonuniform','Nonuniform circular motion',
'When speed changes on a circular path, acceleration has an inward component that changes direction and a tangential component that changes speed. These two components are perpendicular, so their magnitudes combine by Pythagoras.',
'Angular acceleration is the time rate of change of angular velocity. For a fixed radius, it determines tangential acceleration. Keep radial force balance separate from the tangential equation when a body moves around a vertical circle.',
r'a_t=\frac{dv}{dt}=r\alpha,\quad a_r=\frac{v^2}{r},\quad a=\sqrt{a_t^2+a_r^2}',
'a_t and a_r are tangential and inward accelerations (m/s²); r is fixed radius (m); α is angular acceleration (rad/s²); v is speed (m/s); a is total magnitude. The radial and tangential unit directions change with position.',
'The total acceleration is not the ordinary sum a_t+a_r.',
'A body has tangential acceleration 3 m/s² and radial acceleration 4 m/s². Find total acceleration.',
'The perpendicular components give √(9+16)=5 m/s². Its direction makes tan⁻¹(3/4) with the inward radial direction.',
'For constant nonzero speed on a circle, tangential acceleration is',
'Zero|v²/r|v/r|Equal to radial acceleration',0,
'No speed change means dv/dt=0. The radial component remains nonzero because the direction still changes.')

c=chapter('laws','Laws of Motion · Part 1',1,'316–414','Use free-body diagrams to connect forces with changes in momentum. Resolve connected systems, apparent weight and circular-motion forces before adding friction in Part 2.',4)
section(c,'newton','Inertia and Newton’s three laws',
'In an inertial frame, an object maintains its velocity unless a net external force acts. Mass measures resistance to acceleration. Newton’s second law relates net force to momentum change; the familiar ma form assumes constant mass.',
'Action and reaction are equal, opposite forces on different bodies. They cannot cancel on one body’s free-body diagram. They do cancel as internal forces when both bodies belong to the chosen system.',
r'\vec F_{\rm net}=\frac{d\vec p}{dt}=m\vec a\quad(\text{constant }m),\qquad \vec p=m\vec v',
'F_net is sum of external forces (N); p is momentum (kg m/s); m is mass (kg); a acceleration (m/s²); v velocity (m/s). These equations are used in an inertial reference frame.',
'Motion does not require a continuing net force; acceleration does.',
'A 2 kg block experiences 10 N right and 4 N left. Find acceleration.',
'Net force is 6 N right, so a=6/2=3 m/s² right. Use the net force, not the larger individual force.',
'The reaction to Earth’s gravitational pull on a book is',
'The table pushing the book|The book pulling Earth|The book pushing the table|Air pushing the book',1,
'The gravitational pair acts between Earth and book. The normal force from the table belongs to a different contact interaction.')
section(c,'fbd','Free-body diagrams and contact forces',
'Isolate one body and draw only the forces acting on it. Weight acts downward, tension along a taut string, and normal force perpendicular to a contact surface. Label who exerts every force to avoid inventing extra forces.',
'The normal force is determined by acceleration and other forces perpendicular to the surface. It equals mg only in special circumstances. Resolve weight parallel and perpendicular to an incline to simplify the equations.',
r'W=mg,\qquad W_{\parallel}=mg\sin\theta,\qquad W_{\perp}=mg\cos\theta',
'W is gravitational force (N); m mass (kg); g gravity magnitude (m/s²); θ incline angle from horizontal. Parallel weight points downhill; perpendicular weight points into the plane. These are components of one force.',
'Do not draw mg together with both of its components as three separate forces.',
'A 5 kg block rests on a smooth 30° incline. Use g=10 m/s². Find normal force and downhill acceleration.',
'N=50 cos30°=25√3 N. Downhill net force is 50 sin30°=25 N, giving acceleration 5 m/s².',
'A downward applied force of 8 N acts on a stationary 2 kg block on a horizontal table, with g=10. Normal force is',
'12 N|20 N|28 N|8 N',2,
'The table supports both the 20 N weight and the additional 8 N push. Vertical equilibrium gives N=28 N.')
section(c,'connected','Connected masses, pulleys and string constraints',
'A light inextensible string over a smooth massless pulley has the same tension throughout. Its length constraint relates the motions of the connected masses. For a simple fixed pulley, their acceleration magnitudes match.',
'Write a separate force equation for each body, then add to eliminate internal tension. A movable pulley can have two string segments supporting one load, so its displacement relation differs; derive the constraint from total string length.',
r'a=\frac{(m_2-m_1)g}{m_1+m_2},\qquad T=\frac{2m_1m_2g}{m_1+m_2}',
'm₁ and m₂ are masses (kg) with m₂>m₁; a is simple Atwood acceleration magnitude (m/s²); T is string tension (N); g gravity (m/s²). Assumptions: light taut string, fixed smooth massless pulley and negligible drag.',
'Tension is not simply the weight of either accelerating mass.',
'An ideal Atwood machine has masses 2 and 3 kg. Use g=10 m/s². Find acceleration and tension.',
'a=(3−2)10/5=2 m/s². For the rising mass, T−20=2×2, so T=24 N. The other equation gives 30−24=3×2.',
'Two masses in an ideal fixed-pulley Atwood machine have',
'Equal velocities as vectors|Equal acceleration magnitudes|Zero tension|Identical weights',1,
'The fixed total string length makes the magnitudes of their accelerations equal, but their vertical directions are opposite.')
section(c,'lift','Apparent weight and accelerated frames',
'A weighing scale measures its contact normal force, not the gravitational force directly. In an upward-accelerating lift, the scale must provide extra upward force. In a downward-accelerating lift, it provides less.',
'In a frame accelerating with the lift, one may add a pseudo force opposite the frame acceleration to use equilibrium-style equations. This is a frame accounting device, not an interaction from another body.',
r'N=m(g+a)\ \text{(upward acceleration)},\quad N=m(g-a)\ \text{(downward acceleration)},\quad \vec F_{\rm pseudo}=-m\vec a_{\rm frame}',
'N is scale force (N); m mass (kg); a is the positive acceleration magnitude (m/s²); g gravity magnitude. Downward contact formula assumes a≤g and maintained contact. Pseudo force belongs only to the specified non-inertial frame.',
'A lift moving downward can give a larger apparent weight if it is slowing, because its acceleration is upward.',
'A 50 kg person is in a lift descending but slowing at 2 m/s². With g=10, what does the scale read?',
'Acceleration is upward. Thus N=50(10+2)=600 N, equivalent to 60 kg on a scale calibrated with g=10.',
'In an ideal freely falling lift, apparent weight is',
'mg|2mg|Zero|mg/2',2,
'Lift and person both accelerate downward at g, so no normal force is needed. Gravitational force still acts; only the scale reading vanishes.')
section(c,'impulse','Impulse and momentum conservation',
'Impulse is accumulated force over a time interval and equals momentum change. A short impact can have a large force even when its impulse is modest. Using average force connects an impulse to the collision duration.',
'A system conserves total momentum when its net external impulse is negligible. Internal collision forces cancel pairwise. Kinetic energy need not be conserved, so check momentum and energy separately.',
r'\vec J=\int\vec F\,dt=\Delta\vec p,\qquad \vec F_{\rm av}=\frac{\Delta\vec p}{\Delta t},\qquad \vec P_f=\vec P_i\quad(J_{\rm ext}=0)',
'J is impulse (N s); F force (N); p momentum (kg m/s); Δt interaction duration (s); P is total system momentum. Conservation applies to the chosen system when external impulse is zero or negligible.',
'Reversal requires subtracting signed velocities; the momentum change can be twice the initial magnitude.',
'A 0.2 kg ball reverses from +10 to −10 m/s in 0.02 s. Find average force.',
'Δp=0.2(−10−10)=−4 kg m/s. Average force is −4/0.02=−200 N, opposite the initial direction.',
'In a collision with negligible external impulse, which is always conserved?',
'Individual speeds|Total momentum|Total kinetic energy|Individual momentum',1,
'Total momentum follows from zero external impulse. Internal forces change individual momenta, and inelastic collisions change total kinetic energy.')
section(c,'circular','Forces in circular motion and banking',
'Circular motion requires an inward net force. Draw the actual forces and equate their radial component sum to mv²/r. On a level road, friction can provide this inward force; on an ideal banked road, a normal-force component can provide it.',
'For a frictionless banked turn, the normal force’s vertical component balances weight and its horizontal component supplies centripetal force. Dividing these equations gives the design speed. There is no extra centripetal force to draw.',
r'\sum F_r=\frac{mv^2}{r},\qquad \tan\theta=\frac{v^2}{rg}\quad\text{(frictionless bank)}',
'm is mass (kg); v speed (m/s); r turn radius (m); F_r actual force components directed inward (N); θ road-bank angle; g gravity (m/s²). The banking relation assumes no vertical acceleration and no friction.',
'Weight and normal force are not an action–reaction pair; both act on the vehicle.',
'A frictionless banked turn has r=40 m and tanθ=0.25. With g=10, find design speed.',
'v=√(rg tanθ)=√(40×10×0.25)=10 m/s. Mass cancels from both radial and vertical balance.',
'For a frictionless banked road, centripetal force is supplied by',
'A separate new force|The horizontal normal-force component|Gravity alone horizontally|A tangential push',1,
'The normal force is tilted by the bank angle. Its inward horizontal component turns the vehicle while its vertical component balances weight.')

c=chapter('friction','Laws of Motion · Part 2: Friction',2,'1–69','Friction responds to the tendency to slip. Decide whether contact is static or sliding before substituting a coefficient.')
section(c,'static','Static friction is adjustable',
'Static friction prevents relative slipping at the contact. It adjusts to the amount needed for equilibrium, up to a maximum value. The coefficient sets the limit; it does not force friction to equal that limit in every stationary situation.',
'To solve, first assume no slip, find the friction required from the equations, and check whether it is within μₛN. If the requirement exceeds the limit, slipping begins and the static assumption fails.',
r'0\leq |f_s|\leq\mu_sN,\qquad f_{s,\max}=\mu_sN',
'fₛ is signed static friction along the contact (N); μₛ is dimensionless static coefficient; N is normal force (N). Friction opposes the relative sliding tendency, not necessarily the centre-of-mass velocity.',
'At rest with no horizontal force and no slip tendency, friction can be zero.',
'A block has normal force 40 N and μₛ=0.5. A 12 N horizontal force is applied. Find static friction.',
'The maximum available static friction is 20 N. Since only 12 N is required to balance the push, friction is 12 N opposite it and the block remains at rest.',
'If the applied force is 5 N and maximum static friction is 15 N, actual friction at equilibrium is',
'0 N|5 N|15 N|20 N',1,
'Static friction adjusts to the required 5 N. It reaches 15 N only at the threshold of slipping.')
section(c,'kinetic','Kinetic friction and work',
'Once surfaces slide relative to each other, kinetic friction opposes that relative motion. In the elementary model its magnitude is approximately μₖN. It generally converts organised mechanical energy into internal energy.',
'Find the normal force first, including any vertical push or pull. Then use the sliding-force equation along the motion. The kinetic coefficient is often smaller than the static coefficient, though material and speed dependence are more complicated in reality.',
r'f_k=\mu_kN,\qquad W_f=-f_ks\quad\text{(fixed surface, opposite sliding)}',
'fₖ is kinetic friction magnitude (N); μₖ is dimensionless kinetic coefficient; N normal force (N); s is sliding distance (m); W_f is work on the sliding body (J) for constant opposing friction on a stationary surface.',
'Friction can do positive work on a body carried by a moving belt; use the actual force and ground-frame displacement.',
'A 2 kg block slides on a level surface with μₖ=0.2. Use g=10. Find deceleration and work over 3 m.',
'N=20 N and fₖ=4 N. Acceleration opposes motion with magnitude 4/2=2 m/s². Work is −4×3=−12 J.',
'On a horizontal surface with no extra vertical force, sliding deceleration magnitude is',
'μₖmg|μₖg|g/μₖ|μₖ/m',1,
'Friction μₖmg divided by mass gives μₖg. The mass cancels; μₖmg is the force, not the acceleration.')
section(c,'inclines','Inclines, angle of friction and angle of repose',
'On an incline, gravity has a downhill component and normal force is reduced to mg cosθ if no other perpendicular force acts. Static friction must supply mg sinθ to hold the block. Their ratio determines the limiting angle.',
'The angle of repose is the largest slope angle allowing rest in this model. The angle of friction describes the tilt of the combined normal-and-limiting-friction reaction away from the normal. Both have tangent μₛ at the slipping threshold.',
r'\tan\theta_r=\mu_s,\qquad a=g(\sin\theta-\mu_k\cos\theta)\quad\text{(sliding downhill)}',
'θᵣ is repose angle; θ actual incline angle; μₛ and μₖ static/kinetic coefficients; a is downhill acceleration (m/s²); g gravity. The acceleration formula assumes downhill sliding, no other forces and maintained contact.',
'Friction points uphill for downhill sliding, but can point downhill if an external pull tends to move the block uphill.',
'A block has μₛ=1/√3 on an incline. Find its angle of repose.',
'The limiting condition mg sinθ=μₛmg cosθ gives tanθ=1/√3, so θᵣ=30°.',
'A block slides downhill on a 30° plane with μₖ=1/√3. Its acceleration is',
'g/2|g|Zero|g√3/2',2,
'g(sin30°−μₖcos30°)=g(1/2−1/2)=0. It can continue downhill at constant speed; zero acceleration does not mean no motion.')
section(c,'pulling','Pulling at an angle and maximum available friction',
'An upward-angled pull partially supports the block and reduces the normal force. A downward-angled push increases it. Because the friction limit depends on normal force, changing the force direction also changes the threshold for sliding.',
'At impending motion on a level floor, horizontal pull balances limiting friction while vertical forces balance. Solve both together rather than using N=mg. If the normal force reaches zero, the assumed contact model no longer applies.',
r'N=mg-P\sin\theta,\qquad P\cos\theta=\mu_s(mg-P\sin\theta)',
'P is pull magnitude (N), θ angle above horizontal; m mass (kg), g gravity (m/s²); N normal force (N); μₛ static coefficient. Equations describe impending horizontal slip with N≥0 and no vertical acceleration.',
'Using μmg without checking vertical force balance misses the main effect of an angled pull.',
'A 10 kg block is pulled with a 20 N upward vertical component while g=10. If μₛ=0.5, find the horizontal friction limit.',
'The normal force is 100−20=80 N. Maximum static friction is 0.5×80=40 N.',
'An upward-angled pull generally changes normal force by',
'Increasing it|Decreasing it|Leaving it at mg|Making it always zero',1,
'The upward component carries part of the load. Normal force falls by that component until contact is lost.')
section(c,'systems','Friction in connected bodies and turning',
'When stacked blocks move together, static friction can accelerate the upper block. Test whether the required ma is below μₛN. A level-road turn uses the same idea: static friction accelerates the vehicle sideways without sideways sliding.',
'Choose the system to eliminate internal friction when finding common acceleration, then isolate the body that needs friction to determine the no-slip limit. Do not assume that friction on two touching bodies has the same direction; their friction forces are opposite.',
r'a_{\max}=\mu_sg\quad\text{(upper block)},\qquad v_{\max}=\sqrt{\mu_srg}\quad\text{(level turn)}',
'a_max is maximum no-slip acceleration (m/s²) for a horizontally carried block with N=mg; v_max maximum turn speed (m/s); r radius (m); μₛ static coefficient; g gravity. The road relation assumes level motion and no other lateral force.',
'Static friction can point in the direction of a body’s acceleration and do positive work on it.',
'A top block is carried by a lower block accelerating at 3 m/s². With μₛ=0.4 and g=10, will it slip?',
'The no-slip limit is μₛg=4 m/s². Required acceleration is 3, so static friction can carry it without slipping.',
'For μₛ=0.25, r=40 m and g=10, maximum speed on a level circular road is',
'5 m/s|10 m/s|20 m/s|100 m/s',1,
'v_max=√(0.25×40×10)=10 m/s. The quantity inside the root has units m²/s², so 100 is not a speed.')

c=chapter('work','Work, Energy & Power',2,'70–227','Use work to track energy changes, recognise conservative forces and analyse collisions. Every energy equation starts with a clearly chosen system.',5)
section(c,'work','Work by constant and variable forces',
'Work measures energy transfer by a force through displacement of its point of application. Only the force component along the displacement contributes. A force perpendicular to motion can change direction without changing kinetic energy.',
'For a varying one-dimensional force, accumulate small force-times-displacement contributions. The signed area under an F–x graph gives work, including negative contributions where the force opposes displacement.',
r'W=\vec F\cdot\vec s=Fs\cos\theta,\qquad W=\int_{x_i}^{x_f}F_x\,dx',
'W is work (J); F is constant force magnitude (N); s displacement magnitude (m); θ force–displacement angle; Fₓ is the signed force component along x; xᵢ/x_f are integration limits (m). The first formula requires constant force.',
'A large force does zero work if its application point does not move.',
'A 10 N force acts at 60° to a 4 m displacement. Find work.',
'W=10×4×cos60°=20 J. The perpendicular component does no work through that displacement.',
'A force increases linearly from 0 to 12 N over 3 m. Its work is',
'4 J|18 J|36 J|72 J',1,
'The F–x area is a triangle: ½×3×12=18 J. Multiplying final force by distance would incorrectly treat the force as constant.')
section(c,'theorem','Kinetic energy and the work–energy theorem',
'Kinetic energy measures the energy associated with motion in the chosen frame. The work–energy theorem follows by integrating net force along the motion. Net work changes kinetic energy, even when individual forces do different amounts of work.',
'The theorem can avoid finding time or acceleration when speed and displacement are given. Include every force doing work. If the net work is negative, kinetic energy decreases but the final speed must still be found from its square.',
r'K=\tfrac12mv^2,\qquad W_{\rm net}=K_f-K_i',
'K is translational kinetic energy (J); m mass (kg); v speed (m/s); W_net is the sum of works by all forces (J); i/f mean initial/final. The particle form does not include a separate rotational energy.',
'Kinetic energy is frame dependent and nonnegative; momentum has direction.',
'A 2 kg particle speeds up from 3 to 5 m/s. Find net work.',
'ΔK=½×2×(25−9)=16 J. The change is based on the difference of squares, not the square of the speed difference.',
'If a body’s speed triples, its kinetic energy becomes',
'3 times|6 times|9 times|Unchanged',2,
'K scales as v² at fixed mass. Tripling speed gives nine times the energy.')
section(c,'potential','Conservative forces and potential energy',
'A conservative force does work depending only on the endpoints. It allows a potential-energy function whose decrease equals the work done by that force. Near Earth, raising a mass increases gravitational potential energy; stretching a spring stores elastic energy.',
'The zero of potential energy is a chosen reference. Only differences affect the dynamics. In one dimension a negative slope of U(x) means a positive force, so the body tends to move toward lower potential.',
r'W_c=-\Delta U,\quad F_x=-\frac{dU}{dx},\quad U_g=mgh,\quad U_s=\tfrac12kx^2',
'U is potential energy (J); W_c conservative work (J); Fₓ force (N); m mass (kg); g constant gravity; h height above a reference (m); k spring constant (N/m); x spring displacement (m). Spring law assumes Hookean behaviour.',
'Potential energy can be negative depending on the reference; that is not a negative kinetic energy.',
'A 200 N/m spring is stretched by 0.10 m. Find stored energy.',
'U=½×200×0.10²=1 J. Doubling this extension would store 4 J.',
'If U(x)=3x² in SI units, force at x=2 m is',
'+12 N|−12 N|+6 N|−6 N',1,
'Fₓ=−dU/dx=−6x, so at 2 m it is −12 N. The force points toward the lower potential near x=0.')
section(c,'conservation','Mechanical energy and dissipative work',
'With only conservative forces doing work, kinetic plus potential energy remains constant. Gravity can convert potential energy to kinetic energy without changing their sum. Friction reduces mechanical energy while increasing internal energy of the wider system.',
'Choose the system and identify external work before writing an energy balance. A change of mechanical energy equals nonconservative work when conservative interactions are represented by U. Total energy includes the internal energy produced by dissipation.',
r'K_i+U_i+W_{nc}=K_f+U_f',
'K and U are kinetic and potential energies (J); W_nc is work of forces not represented by U (J); i/f are initial/final. Use a consistent reference for potential and include any rotational kinetic energy required by the model.',
'Mechanical-energy loss does not mean total energy disappears.',
'A 2 kg body starts from rest 5 m above ground. Friction dissipates 20 J before arrival. Use g=10. Find final speed.',
'Initial potential is 100 J. Final kinetic is 100−20=80 J, so v=√(2×80/2)=√80≈8.94 m/s.',
'A freely falling body in vacuum has constant',
'Kinetic energy|Potential energy|Total mechanical energy|Vertical momentum',2,
'Gravity decreases potential and increases kinetic energy by equal amounts. Momentum changes under the external gravitational force.')
section(c,'power','Power and efficiency',
'Power is the rate of energy transfer. Average power divides work by the time taken; instantaneous power uses the force and velocity at that instant. A machine can exert a high force at low speed while delivering modest power.',
'Efficiency compares useful output with energy or power supplied. In a constant-power acceleration, force is not constant: with a force parallel to motion, F=P/v decreases as speed rises.',
r'P_{\rm av}=\frac{W}{\Delta t},\quad P=\vec F\cdot\vec v,\quad \eta=\frac{P_{\rm useful}}{P_{\rm input}}',
'P is power (W = J/s); W in the numerator is work (J), not the watt symbol; Δt time (s); F force (N); v velocity (m/s); η is dimensionless efficiency, often expressed as a percentage.',
'Power is a rate, not the amount of energy transferred.',
'A motor raises 20 kg by 3 m in 2 s. Use g=10 and efficiency 75%. Find useful and input powers.',
'Useful work is 600 J, giving 300 W. Input power is 300/0.75=400 W.',
'A parallel force of 50 N moves its application point at 4 m/s. Power is',
'12.5 W|46 W|200 W|800 W',2,
'Instantaneous power is Fv=50×4=200 W. Dividing force by speed has the wrong dimensions.')
section(c,'collisions','Elastic and inelastic collisions',
'During a short collision with negligible external impulse, total momentum is conserved. An elastic collision also conserves total kinetic energy. In a completely inelastic collision, the objects stick together; their final common velocity follows from momentum.',
'The coefficient of restitution describes the relative separation speed compared with approach speed along the collision axis. A zero value means no relative separation immediately after impact, but does not by itself describe every later interaction.',
r'm_1u_1+m_2u_2=m_1v_1+m_2v_2,\quad e=\frac{v_2-v_1}{u_1-u_2},\quad V_{\rm stick}=\frac{m_1u_1+m_2u_2}{m_1+m_2}',
'm₁/m₂ are masses (kg); u₁/u₂ initial and v₁/v₂ final signed velocities (m/s); e is dimensionless restitution for a one-dimensional approach with u₁>u₂; V_stick is common velocity. Momentum requires negligible external impulse.',
'Conserved momentum does not imply conserved kinetic energy.',
'A 2 kg cart at 6 m/s sticks to a stationary 1 kg cart. Find final speed and kinetic-energy loss.',
'Common speed is (2×6)/3=4 m/s. Initial K=36 J and final K=½×3×16=24 J, so 12 J becomes internal energy.',
'Two identical masses collide elastically head-on, one initially at rest. After collision they',
'Stick together|Exchange velocities|Both stop|Both keep their original velocities',1,
'Momentum and kinetic-energy conservation give the moving mass’s speed to the initially stationary mass. Sticking would be inelastic.')
section(c,'vertical-loop','Vertical circles and minimum launch energy',
'A body on a light string in a vertical circle must have enough speed at the top to keep the string taut. At the limiting case, gravity alone supplies the inward acceleration and tension just becomes zero.',
'Between bottom and top the body rises by 2r, so kinetic energy decreases by 2mgr. Combining this energy change with the top contact condition gives the minimum bottom speed. A rigid rod can push as well as pull and has a different constraint.',
r'v_{\rm top,min}=\sqrt{gr},\qquad v_{\rm bottom,min}=\sqrt{5gr}',
'v is speed (m/s); g gravity (m/s²); r circle radius (m). These limits apply to a point mass on a taut light string making a complete vertical circle without dissipative losses; the bottom result assumes energy conservation.',
'Do not use the light-string minimum for a rigid rod or a track that can maintain contact differently.',
'A string circle has radius 0.8 m and g=10. Find the minimum bottom speed.',
'v=√(5×10×0.8)=√40≈6.32 m/s. At the top, minimum speed is √8≈2.83 m/s.',
'At the top in the limiting complete string circle, tension is',
'mg|2mg|Zero|5mg',2,
'At the threshold, mg=mv²/r and no extra inward tension is needed. Below that speed the string would slacken.')

c=chapter('rotation','System of Particles & Rotational Motion',2,'228–405','Track the centre of mass, then distinguish translation from rotation. Specify an axis whenever using torque, angular momentum or moment of inertia.',6)
section(c,'centre','Centre of mass and its motion',
'The centre of mass is the mass-weighted average position. It can lie in empty space, as for a uniform ring. Internal motions can change the positions of the parts while leaving the centre-of-mass motion controlled by external forces.',
'Choose a convenient origin and use signed coordinates. A uniform object’s symmetry often locates its centre immediately; removing a piece can be handled as subtracting its mass contribution from the original object.',
r'\vec R=\frac{\sum m_i\vec r_i}{M},\quad M=\sum m_i,\quad \vec F_{\rm ext}=M\vec A_{CM}',
'R is centre-of-mass position (m); mᵢ masses (kg); rᵢ positions (m); M total mass (kg); A_CM centre-of-mass acceleration (m/s²); F_ext total external force (N). The last equation assumes a fixed-mass system.',
'The centre of mass need not be inside the material or at the geometric centre of an uneven object.',
'Masses 2 and 3 kg are at x=0 and x=10 m. Find their centre of mass.',
'X=(2×0+3×10)/(2+3)=6 m. It lies closer to the heavier mass.',
'Two equal masses are at x=−4 and +2 m. Their centre is',
'−1 m|+1 m|−2 m|+3 m',0,
'For equal masses the centre is the average coordinate: (−4+2)/2=−1 m. Averaging distances from the origin would lose the signs.')
section(c,'angular','Angular variables and fixed-axis kinematics',
'Every point of a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis shares the same angular displacement, angular velocity and angular acceleration. Its linear speed depends on its perpendicular distance from that axis.',
'The constant-angular-acceleration equations mirror linear kinematics. Use radians when relating angles to arc length. A point on the axis has zero tangential speed even though the body has nonzero angular velocity.',
r'\omega=\omega_0+\alpha t,\quad \theta=\omega_0t+\tfrac12\alpha t^2,\quad v=r\omega,\quad a_t=r\alpha',
'ω₀/ω are initial/final angular velocities (rad/s); α constant angular acceleration (rad/s²); θ angular displacement (rad); t time (s); r perpendicular axis distance (m); v tangential speed (m/s); a_t tangential acceleration.',
'Points at different radii share angular speed, not linear speed.',
'A wheel starts from rest with α=2 rad/s² for 3 s. Find ω and angular displacement.',
'ω=6 rad/s and θ=½×2×9=9 rad. A point 0.5 m from the axis then has speed 3 m/s.',
'For a rigid rotating disc, the point at twice the radius has',
'Twice the angular speed|Half the linear speed|Twice the linear speed|The same linear speed',2,
'v=rω and all points share ω, so doubling radius doubles linear speed.')
section(c,'torque','Torque, couples and equilibrium',
'Torque measures the turning effect of a force about a specified point or axis. It depends on the perpendicular lever arm, not simply the distance from the axis. A force directed through the axis produces no torque.',
'A couple consists of equal opposite forces with separate lines of action: net force vanishes but their torques add. A rigid body in static equilibrium needs both force balance and torque balance.',
r'\vec\tau=\vec r\times\vec F,\quad |\tau|=Fd_\perp,\quad \sum\vec F=0,\quad \sum\vec\tau=0',
'r is position from chosen origin to force application (m); F force (N); d_⊥ perpendicular distance to its line of action (m); τ torque (N m). Both balance equations are needed for static rigid-body equilibrium.',
'Torque has the same base dimensions as energy but describes a different physical quantity.',
'A 40 N force acts perpendicular to a spanner 0.25 m from its axis. Find torque.',
'τ=40×0.25=10 N m. If applied along the spanner toward the axis, its torque would be zero.',
'Equal opposite forces separated by 0.4 m, each 10 N, form a couple with torque',
'0 N m|2 N m|4 N m|8 N m',2,
'Couple torque is one force magnitude times the perpendicular separation: 10×0.4=4 N m. The forces cancel translationally but their turning effects add.')
section(c,'inertia','Moment of inertia and standard shapes',
'Moment of inertia is rotational resistance for a specified axis. Mass farther from the axis contributes more because its distance is squared. The same object therefore has different moments of inertia about different axes.',
'A ring puts all its mass at radius R, while a disc distributes some nearer the centre. Common uniform-body results are useful only with their axes stated. For an irregular distribution, sum or integrate the squared distances.',
r'I=\sum m_ir_i^2,\quad I_{\rm ring}=MR^2,\quad I_{\rm disc}=\tfrac12MR^2,\quad I_{\rm sphere}=\tfrac25MR^2,\quad I_{\rm rod,CM}=\tfrac1{12}M\ell^2',
'I is moment of inertia (kg m²); mᵢ mass elements (kg); rᵢ perpendicular axis distances (m); M total mass; R radius; ℓ rod length. Ring/disc axes are central perpendicular axes; solid-sphere axis is a diameter; rod axis is perpendicular through its midpoint. All shapes are uniform.',
'Always name the axis; a memorised shape formula without its axis can be wrong.',
'A uniform disc has M=2 kg and R=0.5 m. Find I about its central perpendicular axis.',
'I=½×2×0.5²=0.25 kg m². A thin ring with the same M and R would have 0.5 kg m².',
'For equal mass and radius, a thin ring’s central-axis inertia is how many times a disc’s?',
'Half|Equal|Twice|Four times',2,
'The ring has MR² and the disc has MR²/2, so the ratio is 2.')
section(c,'axis','Parallel-axis and perpendicular-axis theorems',
'The parallel-axis theorem shifts from a centre-of-mass axis to a parallel axis. The added term reflects the translational offset of the whole mass distribution. It does not connect two arbitrarily oriented axes.',
'For a flat lamina, the perpendicular-axis theorem relates the out-of-plane inertia to two in-plane inertias through the same point. It is restricted to planar mass distributions and must not be applied to a three-dimensional sphere.',
r'I=I_{CM}+Md^2,\qquad I_z=I_x+I_y\quad\text{(plane lamina in xy)}',
'I/I_CM are inertias (kg m²) about parallel axes; M mass (kg); d separation (m). For the second theorem, x and y lie in the lamina and z is perpendicular, all through one point.',
'The starting axis for the Md² shift must pass through the centre of mass.',
'A uniform rod has midpoint inertia Mℓ²/12. Find inertia about a perpendicular axis through one end.',
'The axes are separated by ℓ/2. Thus I=Mℓ²/12+M(ℓ/2)²=Mℓ²/3.',
'A body has I_CM=2 kg m², M=3 kg and a parallel axis 2 m away. Its inertia is',
'5 kg m²|8 kg m²|14 kg m²|20 kg m²',2,
'Add Md²=3×4=12 to 2, giving 14 kg m². The distance must be squared.')
section(c,'dynamics','Rotational dynamics, energy and power',
'For rotation about a fixed principal axis, net torque changes angular velocity according to τ=Iα. Torque is the rotational counterpart of force and moment of inertia the counterpart of mass, but their physical units differ.',
'Rotational kinetic energy sums the kinetic energies of all mass elements. Work done by an axial torque accumulates over angular displacement. The scalar formulas below assume the rotation and torques are resolved along the specified fixed axis.',
r'\tau_{\rm net}=I\alpha,\quad K_{\rm rot}=\tfrac12I\omega^2,\quad W=\int\tau\,d\theta,\quad P=\tau\omega',
'τ is axial torque (N m); I constant axis inertia (kg m²); α angular acceleration (rad/s²); ω angular velocity (rad/s); K_rot and W energy/work (J); θ angle (rad); P power (W). Fixed-axis rigid rotation is assumed.',
'If an object also translates, add its centre-of-mass kinetic energy.',
'A wheel with I=0.5 kg m² experiences net torque 2 N m. Find angular acceleration and energy at ω=4 rad/s.',
'α=2/0.5=4 rad/s². K_rot=½×0.5×16=4 J.',
'A rotor with I=2 kg m² spins at 3 rad/s. Its rotational kinetic energy is',
'3 J|6 J|9 J|18 J',2,
'K=½Iω²=½×2×9=9 J. Multiplying I by ω gives angular momentum instead.')
section(c,'momentum','Angular momentum and conservation',
'Angular momentum for a particle is its position crossed with momentum about an origin. A particle travelling in a straight line can have angular momentum about a point away from that line. For fixed-axis rigid rotation it reduces to Iω along the axis.',
'External torque is the rate of change of total angular momentum. With negligible external torque, pulling mass closer to the axis decreases I and increases ω. Energy may change if muscles or a motor do work during the rearrangement.',
r'\vec L=\vec r\times\vec p,\quad L=I\omega,\quad \tau_{\rm ext}=\frac{dL}{dt},\quad I_i\omega_i=I_f\omega_f\quad(\tau_{\rm ext}=0)',
'L is angular momentum (kg m²/s); r position from origin (m); p momentum (kg m/s); I inertia about the fixed axis; ω angular velocity. Scalar conservation applies along that axis with negligible external torque.',
'Conserved angular momentum does not require conserved rotational kinetic energy when I changes.',
'A skater’s inertia falls from 4 to 1 kg m² with initial angular speed 2 rad/s and no external torque. Find final speed.',
'4×2=1×ω_f, so ω_f=8 rad/s. Rotational energy rises from 8 to 32 J, supplied by work during the pull inward.',
'With angular momentum fixed, halving I makes rotational kinetic energy',
'Half|Unchanged|Twice|Four times',2,
'K=L²/(2I), so halving I doubles K. The increased energy must come from work by the mechanism changing the mass distribution.')
section(c,'rolling','Rolling without slipping',
'Pure rolling combines centre-of-mass translation with rotation. The contact point is instantaneously at rest relative to a stationary surface, giving v_CM=ωR. The top point of the rolling body moves at twice the centre speed in the ground frame.',
'A rolling body’s energy includes both translation and rotation. On a fixed ideal surface, static friction can enforce rolling without dissipative sliding work. The downhill acceleration depends on how the mass is distributed.',
r'v_{CM}=\omega R,\quad K=\tfrac12Mv_{CM}^2+\tfrac12I_{CM}\omega^2,\quad a=\frac{g\sin\theta}{1+I_{CM}/(MR^2)}',
'M mass (kg); R rolling radius (m); v_CM centre speed (m/s); ω angular speed (rad/s); I_CM inertia about centre axis (kg m²); θ incline angle; a downhill acceleration. Assumes sufficient static friction, no slipping and no rolling resistance.',
'If the contact slips, v_CM=ωR is no longer valid.',
'A uniform solid sphere rolls down an incline with sinθ=0.5 and g=10. Find acceleration.',
'I/(MR²)=2/5. Thus a=5/(1+2/5)=25/7≈3.57 m/s², less than a frictionless sliding particle’s 5 m/s².',
'A disc rolling at centre speed v has total kinetic energy',
'Mv²/2|3Mv²/4|Mv²|3Mv²/2',1,
'Translation gives Mv²/2. With I=MR²/2 and ω=v/R, rotation gives Mv²/4. Sum is 3Mv²/4.')

c=chapter('gravitation','Gravitation',3,'1–97','Connect force, field and potential, then derive orbital motion and escape speed. Distinguish distance from Earth’s centre from height above its surface.',7)
section(c,'field','Universal gravitation and field superposition',
'Every pair of masses attracts with an inverse-square force. The gravitational field describes force per unit test mass, allowing source masses to be analysed independently of the object placed in the field.',
'For several sources, fields add as vectors. Between two masses their fields oppose; outside the pair they may reinforce. For a spherical symmetric source, the external field equals that of a point mass at its centre.',
r'F=\frac{GMm}{r^2},\qquad g_r=\frac{GM}{r^2},\qquad \vec g_{\rm net}=\sum\vec g_i',
'G is gravitational constant (N m²/kg²); M source mass and m test mass (kg); r centre separation (m); F attractive force (N); g_r field magnitude (m/s²). Point masses or an external spherical field are assumed.',
'Fields are vectors; adding magnitudes without directions can miss a cancellation.',
'If the separation of two fixed masses triples, how does the force change?',
'The inverse-square factor is 1/3²=1/9, so force falls to one ninth.',
'Two equal masses lie equally far on opposite sides of a midpoint. The field there is',
'Zero|Twice one field|Half one field|Infinite',0,
'Equal field magnitudes point in opposite directions at the midpoint. Their vector sum is zero even though the gravitational potential there is negative.')
section(c,'variation','Gravity above and below the surface',
'Above a spherical Earth, field strength decreases with the square of distance from the centre. Height h adds to the Earth radius rather than replacing it. For heights much smaller than R, the leading fractional decrease is 2h/R.',
'Inside a uniform-density sphere, only the mass enclosed within radius r contributes to the field. That enclosed mass scales as r³, so the field scales as r and vanishes at the centre. Real Earth’s nonuniform density makes the linear depth law an idealisation.',
r'g_h=g_0\left(\frac{R}{R+h}\right)^2,\qquad g_d=g_0\left(1-\frac dR\right)',
'g₀ surface gravity (m/s²); R Earth radius (m); h height above surface (m); d depth below surface (m); g_h/g_d gravity magnitudes. The depth formula assumes a uniform, nonrotating spherical Earth; the height formula assumes spherical symmetry.',
'Depth dependence is not an inverse-square law using R−d with the full Earth mass.',
'At height h=R, find gravity in terms of surface value.',
'Distance from the centre is 2R, so g_h=g₀(R/2R)²=g₀/4.',
'Inside a uniform sphere at depth R/2, gravity is',
'g₀/4|g₀/2|2g₀|4g₀',1,
'Use the uniform-sphere depth relation: g_d=g₀(1−1/2)=g₀/2. The enclosed source mass has decreased.')
section(c,'potential','Gravitational potential and energy',
'Potential is gravitational potential energy per unit mass. With zero chosen at infinity, it is negative near an attractive mass because energy must be supplied to remove the test mass to infinity.',
'Potentials add as scalars while fields add as vectors. A shell produces zero field inside but a constant nonzero potential there. Zero slope of potential means zero field, not necessarily zero potential.',
r'V=-\frac{GM}{r},\quad U=mV=-\frac{GMm}{r},\quad g_r=-\frac{dV}{dr}\ \text{(signed outward component)}',
'V is potential (J/kg); U potential energy (J); G gravitational constant; M source and m test masses (kg); r centre distance (m). Zero is at infinity; outside a spherical mass or for point masses. The signed field is inward.',
'Negative potential is consistent with positive work needed to escape.',
'What is the potential-energy change when a mass moves from r=R to r=2R?',
r'\(\Delta U=-GMm/(2R)+GMm/R=GMm/(2R)\), positive. An external agent must supply this energy in a slow lift.',
'Inside a uniform thin spherical shell, gravitational field and potential are',
'Both zero|Zero field and constant negative potential|Nonzero field and zero potential|Both varying with radius',1,
'Opposite shell contributions cancel in the field. Their negative scalar potentials add to the constant −GM/R inside.')
section(c,'orbits','Circular satellites and orbital energy',
'A satellite in circular orbit is continuously falling toward Earth while its tangential motion carries it around. Gravity supplies centripetal acceleration. The satellite mass cancels, so circular speed depends on the source mass and orbital radius.',
'The orbital total energy is negative, indicating a bound system. Kinetic energy is positive and half the magnitude of potential energy. Apparent weightlessness arises from free fall, not the absence of gravity.',
r'v_o=\sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}},\quad T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}},\quad E=-\frac{GMm}{2r},\quad K=\frac{GMm}{2r}',
'v_o orbital speed (m/s); T period (s); G constant; M central mass (kg); m satellite mass (kg); r circular centre-to-centre radius (m); E total and K kinetic energies (J). Ignore drag and the satellite’s effect on the source.',
'Use r=R+h; altitude alone is not the orbit radius.',
'If a circular orbital radius quadruples, find speed and period ratios.',
'Speed scales as r⁻¹/², so it halves. Period scales as r³/², so it grows by 4³/²=8.',
'For a circular gravitational orbit, total energy E relative to kinetic energy K is',
'E=K|E=−K|E=−2K|E=0',1,
'Potential energy is −2K, so E=K−2K=−K. Zero total energy instead describes the escape threshold.')
section(c,'escape','Escape speed and gravitational binding',
'Escape speed is the minimum launch speed to reach infinity with no residual speed when only gravity acts. Set the initial kinetic plus gravitational potential energy equal to zero. It is not the speed needed to remain in a circular orbit.',
'Escape speed does not depend on the escaping object’s mass. At a given radius it is √2 times circular speed. Drag, rotation, propulsion during flight and other celestial bodies change the practical launch problem.',
r'v_e=\sqrt{\frac{2GM}{r}}=\sqrt{2g_0R}\quad\text{(surface }r=R)',
'v_e escape speed (m/s); G constant; M source mass (kg); r initial centre distance (m); g₀ surface gravity; R source radius. Zero potential is at infinity; ignore drag and subsequent propulsion.',
'Escape speed is a local threshold and decreases when the starting radius increases.',
'A planet has surface g=5 m/s² and radius 4×10⁶ m. Find surface escape speed.',
'v_e=√(2×5×4×10⁶)=√(4×10⁷)≈6325 m/s, or 6.32 km/s.',
'At the same radius, escape speed divided by circular orbital speed is',
'1/√2|1|√2|2',2,
'The escape expression has 2GM/r under the root while circular speed has GM/r, giving √2.')
section(c,'kepler','Kepler’s laws and geostationary satellites',
'An inverse-square central force permits elliptical bound orbits with the source at a focus. Equal swept areas in equal times express conserved angular momentum: a satellite moves faster when closer to the source.',
'Kepler’s third law connects period with the semi-major axis. A geostationary satellite must be in a circular equatorial orbit, move with Earth’s rotation, and share its sidereal rotation period; matching period alone is not enough.',
r'T^2=\frac{4\pi^2}{GM}a^3,\qquad \frac{dA}{dt}=\text{constant}',
'T is orbital period (s); a is ellipse semi-major axis (m), not acceleration; G constant; M dominant central mass (kg); A swept area (m²). The test-body approximation neglects orbiting mass compared with M.',
'An orbit inclined to the equator with a 24-hour period is not stationary in the sky.',
'For orbits around the same star, the second has semi-major axis 9 times the first. Find its period ratio.',
'T₂/T₁=(a₂/a₁)³/²=9³/²=27. Eccentricity does not change this period relation for the same semi-major axis.',
'In an elliptical orbit, a satellite’s speed is greatest',
'Farthest from the source|Nearest the source|At every point equally|Only when its acceleration vanishes',1,
'Conserved swept-area rate and angular momentum require higher speed near the source. Gravity remains nonzero throughout the orbit.')

c=chapter('oscillations','Oscillations',3,'293–395','Start with restoring forces and phase, then connect displacement, velocity and energy. Distinguish ideal SHM from damped and driven motion.',13)
section(c,'periodic','Periodic motion and the SHM condition',
'Oscillation is repeated motion about an equilibrium position. Periodic motion repeats after a fixed time, but not every periodic motion is simple harmonic. SHM requires acceleration proportional to displacement and directed toward equilibrium.',
'The minus sign in a=−ω²x states the restoring direction. A linear spring provides this force while it remains Hookean. A pendulum approximates it only for small angular displacement.',
r'a=-\omega^2x,\qquad F=-kx,\qquad \omega=\sqrt{k/m}',
'x is displacement from stable equilibrium (m); a acceleration (m/s²); ω angular frequency (rad/s); F restoring force (N); k positive stiffness (N/m); m mass (kg). The spring relation assumes a linear restoring force.',
'A force directed toward equilibrium is insufficient for SHM unless its magnitude is proportional to displacement.',
'A 0.5 kg mass has restoring force F=−8x in SI units. Find angular frequency.',
'k=8 N/m. Thus ω=√(8/0.5)=4 rad/s. The negative force coefficient indicates restoration.',
'Which acceleration law represents stable SHM?',
'a=+4x|a=−4x|a=−4x²|a=−4',1,
'a=−4x has ω²=4 with the required proportionality and restoring sign. Positive proportional acceleration drives the body away.')
section(c,'phase','Displacement, phase and initial conditions',
'A sine or cosine describes SHM. Amplitude is maximum displacement from equilibrium; phase states where the body is within its cycle. The phase constant encodes its initial position and direction of motion.',
'One full phase increase of 2π corresponds to one period. A cosine beginning at maximum positive displacement has zero initial velocity; starting at equilibrium generally requires another phase. Determine phase from both position and velocity, not position alone.',
r'x=A\cos(\omega t+\phi),\qquad T=\frac{2\pi}{\omega},\qquad f=\frac1T',
'x displacement and A amplitude (m); ω angular frequency (rad/s); t time (s); φ initial phase (rad); T period (s); f frequency (Hz). The equilibrium coordinate is x=0.',
'Frequency f and angular frequency ω differ by 2π.',
'For x=0.04 cos(5πt) m, find amplitude, frequency and period.',
'A=0.04 m and ω=5π rad/s. Hence f=ω/(2π)=2.5 Hz and T=0.4 s.',
'Two oscillations with the same frequency differing in phase by π are',
'In phase|In opposite phase|A quarter cycle apart|Of different period',1,
'π is half a full 2π cycle. Their displacements have opposite signs at corresponding times if amplitudes and equilibrium references match.')
section(c,'velocity','Velocity and acceleration through a cycle',
'In SHM, speed is greatest at equilibrium and zero at the turning points. Acceleration is zero at equilibrium and largest in magnitude at the extremes. Velocity and acceleration are not simultaneously maximal.',
'Differentiating displacement gives a quarter-cycle phase shift in velocity and a half-cycle reversal in acceleration. The displacement–speed relation determines speed but not the direction; use the part of the cycle to choose the sign.',
r'v=-A\omega\sin(\omega t+\phi),\quad v^2=\omega^2(A^2-x^2),\quad a=-\omega^2x',
'v signed velocity (m/s); A amplitude and x displacement (m); ω angular frequency (rad/s); t time (s); φ phase (rad); a acceleration (m/s²). The relation gives |v| and allows two directions at an interior position.',
'At an extreme, zero velocity does not imply zero acceleration.',
'For A=0.10 m and ω=20 rad/s, find speed at x=0.06 m.',
'|v|=20√(0.10²−0.06²)=20×0.08=1.6 m/s. Direction depends on whether the particle is moving toward or away from the positive extreme.',
'At equilibrium in ideal SHM, speed and acceleration are',
'Both zero|Maximum speed and zero acceleration|Zero speed and maximum acceleration|Both maximum',1,
'At x=0 the restoring acceleration vanishes, while all mechanical energy is kinetic and speed is maximal.')
section(c,'energy','Energy exchange in SHM',
'A linear oscillator stores potential energy as it moves away from equilibrium. On returning, that energy becomes kinetic. In ideal undamped SHM, the total remains constant and is proportional to amplitude squared.',
'Energy depends on x², so the same displacement magnitude has the same kinetic and potential energies on either side. Equal energies occur at |x|=A/√2, rather than halfway between equilibrium and an extreme.',
r'E=\tfrac12kA^2,\quad U=\tfrac12kx^2,\quad K=\tfrac12k(A^2-x^2)',
'E total, U potential and K kinetic energies (J); k stiffness (N/m); A amplitude and x displacement (m). Set U=0 at equilibrium and assume no damping or driving.',
'At x=A/2, potential energy is E/4, not E/2.',
'A spring with k=100 N/m oscillates with A=0.20 m. Find total energy and energies at x=0.10 m.',
'E=½×100×0.04=2 J. U=½×100×0.01=0.5 J and K=1.5 J.',
'If an SHM amplitude doubles at fixed k, total energy becomes',
'Twice|Four times|Half|Unchanged',1,
'E depends on A². Doubling amplitude quadruples energy while leaving the ideal linear-spring period unchanged.')
section(c,'springs','Spring systems and shifted equilibrium',
'A spring-mass oscillator’s period depends on mass and effective stiffness. Gravity shifts a vertical spring’s equilibrium extension but does not alter the period about that equilibrium in the ideal linear model.',
'Parallel springs undergo the same extension and their forces add. Series springs carry the same force and their extensions add. First find the effective spring constant, then measure the oscillation displacement from the new equilibrium.',
r'T=2\pi\sqrt{m/k_{\rm eff}},\quad k_{\parallel}=k_1+k_2,\quad \frac1{k_{\rm series}}=\frac1{k_1}+\frac1{k_2},\quad x_0=\frac{mg}{k}',
'T period (s); m mass (kg); k/k_eff/k₁/k₂ stiffnesses (N/m); x₀ static vertical extension (m); g gravity. Springs are light, linear and arranged for the stated shared force or extension.',
'Use displacement from equilibrium in F=−kx, not the total vertical extension from the natural length.',
'Two springs each of 80 N/m are connected in series to a 1 kg mass. Find period.',
'k_eff=40 N/m. T=2π√(1/40)≈0.993 s. Connecting them in parallel would give k_eff=160 N/m and half this period.',
'For the same spring, quadrupling the attached mass changes period by',
'No change|A factor of 2|A factor of 4|A factor of 1/2',1,
'T∝√m at fixed k, so four times the mass gives twice the period.')
section(c,'pendulum','Simple pendulum and effective gravity',
'A simple pendulum consists of a small bob on a light string. The tangential restoring force is −mg sinθ. For small θ in radians, sinθ≈θ makes the angular motion harmonic.',
'The period depends on length and gravity, not bob mass. Large amplitudes invalidate the small-angle period. In an accelerating support frame, replace gravity with the magnitude of effective gravity and measure displacement about the shifted equilibrium direction.',
r'T=2\pi\sqrt{\ell/g},\qquad g_{\rm eff}=g+a\quad\text{(lift accelerating upward)}',
'T period (s); ℓ pivot-to-bob length (m); g gravity magnitude (m/s²); a upward lift acceleration (m/s²); g_eff effective magnitude. Assume small swings, a light inextensible string, a point bob and negligible drag.',
'In ideal free fall the restoring effective gravity vanishes; the usual pendulum oscillation model breaks down.',
'A pendulum has period 2 s. If its length increases to four times its value, find the new period.',
'T∝√ℓ, so the period doubles to 4 s. The bob’s mass is irrelevant in this approximation.',
'An upward-accelerating lift makes a small-angle pendulum’s period',
'Longer|Shorter|Unchanged|Independent of length',1,
'Effective gravity is g+a, increasing the restoring acceleration. Since T∝1/√g_eff, the period decreases.')
section(c,'resonance','Damping, driving and resonance',
'Damping transfers oscillator energy to the surroundings and reduces amplitude. A driving force periodically supplies energy. After transients decay, the forced motion follows the driving frequency rather than freely choosing the natural frequency.',
'Resonance occurs when the driving frequency is near the natural frequency and produces a large response, limited by damping. Greater damping broadens and lowers the resonance response. The ideal undamped formula does not predict a finite steady-state resonant amplitude.',
r'm\ddot x+b\dot x+kx=F_0\cos(\omega_dt),\qquad \omega_0=\sqrt{k/m}',
'x displacement (m); dots time derivatives; m mass (kg); b viscous damping coefficient (kg/s); k stiffness (N/m); F₀ drive amplitude (N); ω_d drive and ω₀ undamped natural angular frequencies (rad/s). This is a linear damped driven model.',
'Forced oscillation frequency is the drive frequency after transients; amplitude depends strongly on the frequency match.',
'Why does pushing a swing at regular well-timed intervals increase its amplitude?',
'Each push can add energy in phase with the motion. Near the swing’s natural frequency, repeated energy input builds amplitude until dissipative loss balances input or the linear approximation fails.',
'Compared with weak damping, strong damping usually makes a resonance peak',
'Higher and narrower|Lower and broader|Infinitely high|Independent of driving frequency',1,
'More damping dissipates energy more rapidly, reducing peak amplitude and spreading the response over a wider frequency range.')

c=chapter('waves','Waves',4,'1–104','Follow a disturbance travelling through a medium while its particles oscillate locally. Connect wave equations, superposition, standing modes, beats and the Doppler effect.',14)
section(c,'travelling','Travelling waves and particle motion',
'A mechanical wave transfers energy through coupled particles; the particles generally oscillate about their own positions. Transverse displacement is perpendicular to propagation, while longitudinal displacement is parallel, as in ordinary sound in air.',
'The phase identifies equivalent points on successive oscillations. In y=A sin(kx−ωt+φ), a fixed phase moves toward increasing x. Changing −ωt to +ωt reverses propagation. Particle velocity is a time derivative, not the wave speed.',
r'y=A\sin(kx-\omega t+\phi),\quad k=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda},\quad \omega=2\pi f,\quad v_w=\frac{\omega}{k}=f\lambda',
'y particle displacement and A amplitude (m); x position (m); t time (s); k wave number (rad/m); λ wavelength (m); ω angular frequency (rad/s); f frequency (Hz); v_w propagation speed (m/s); φ phase (rad).',
'Wave speed and maximum particle speed Aω are different quantities.',
'A wave is y=0.02 sin(4πx−20πt) in SI units. Find wavelength, frequency and speed.',
'k=4π gives λ=0.5 m. ω=20π gives f=10 Hz. Speed is fλ=5 m/s, toward +x.',
'A wave y=A sin(kx+ωt) with k, ω positive travels',
'Toward +x|Toward −x|Only vertically|In neither direction',1,
'Keeping kx+ωt constant requires x to decrease as t increases. The oscillation direction is not the propagation direction.')
section(c,'speed','Wave speed on strings and in gases',
'A stretched string transmits transverse disturbances through its tension. More tension strengthens the restoring interaction; more mass per length increases inertia. Sound in a gas depends on compressional stiffness and density.',
'Sound compressions are approximately adiabatic for ordinary rapid acoustic oscillations, so the gas bulk stiffness is γp rather than p. At a fixed gas composition, sound speed scales with the square root of absolute temperature.',
r'v_{\rm string}=\sqrt{\frac{F_T}{\mu}},\qquad v_{\rm sound}=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma p}{\rho}}=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma RT}{M}}',
'F_T string tension (N); μ mass per length (kg/m); γ gas heat-capacity ratio; p gas pressure (Pa); ρ density (kg/m³); R gas constant (J/mol K); T absolute temperature (K); M molar mass (kg/mol); speeds in m/s. Ideal-gas sound is assumed.',
'Use linear density for a string, not its bulk mass density.',
'A string has tension 100 N and μ=0.01 kg/m. Find wave speed.',
'v=√(100/0.01)=100 m/s. Quadrupling tension at fixed μ would double the speed.',
'For the same ideal gas, increasing temperature from 300 to 1200 K changes sound speed by',
'A factor of 4|A factor of 2|A factor of 1/2|No change',1,
'Sound speed is proportional to √T. The absolute temperature quadruples, so speed doubles.')
section(c,'superposition','Superposition, interference and intensity',
'In a linear medium, overlapping displacements add algebraically. Waves reinforce when their phases match and cancel when opposite. The particles respond to the total displacement; the waves continue through each other in the linear model.',
'Intensity measures average energy flow per unit area and scales with amplitude squared for the same medium and frequency. A fixed phase difference requires coherent waves for a stable interference pattern.',
r'y_{\rm net}=y_1+y_2,\quad A_R^2=A_1^2+A_2^2+2A_1A_2\cos\delta,\quad I\propto A^2',
'y displacements (m); A₁/A₂ component amplitudes (m); A_R resultant amplitude; δ phase difference (rad); I intensity (W/m²). Amplitude formula assumes same frequency and propagation direction at the point considered; intensity scaling holds for fixed medium and frequency.',
'Intensity is not generally the sum of individual intensities where coherent waves overlap.',
'Two equal-amplitude waves A meet in phase. Find resultant amplitude and intensity relative to one wave.',
'A_R=2A. Intensity scales as (2A)², so it is four times that of one wave, not twice.',
'Two equal-amplitude coherent waves with phase difference π produce resultant amplitude',
'0|A|2A|√2A',0,
'cosπ=−1, so A_R²=A²+A²−2A²=0 at that point. This is destructive interference.')
section(c,'standing','Reflection and standing waves',
'A wave reflecting at a fixed string end reverses displacement phase; at a free end it does not. Two equal opposite-travelling waves of the same frequency can form a standing pattern with fixed nodes and antinodes.',
'At a displacement node the medium never moves; at an antinode its amplitude is maximal. Adjacent nodes are λ/2 apart and a node-to-neighbouring-antinode distance is λ/4. In an ideal standing wave the average net energy flow is zero.',
r'y=2A\sin(kx)\cos(\omega t),\quad \Delta x_{NN}=\frac\lambda2,\quad \Delta x_{NA}=\frac\lambda4',
'y displacement (m); A amplitude of each travelling component (m); k wave number (rad/m); ω angular frequency (rad/s); λ wavelength (m); Δx_NN adjacent-node spacing; Δx_NA adjacent node–antinode spacing. Origin is a node.',
'For sound, a displacement node corresponds to a pressure antinode.',
'Adjacent nodes are 0.30 m apart. Find wavelength and nearest node–antinode distance.',
'λ=0.60 m. The nearest node–antinode distance is λ/4=0.15 m.',
'At a standing-wave displacement node, the particle has',
'Maximum displacement|Zero displacement at all times|Maximum amplitude|A moving node position',1,
'The spatial sine factor vanishes at a node, making displacement zero throughout the cycle.')
section(c,'modes','String and air-column harmonics',
'Boundary conditions select wavelengths that fit the resonator. A string fixed at both ends has displacement nodes at both ends; an open air-column end is approximately a displacement antinode, while a closed end is a node.',
'An open–open pipe supports all integer harmonics, like a fixed–fixed string. An open–closed pipe fits odd quarter-wavelengths and supports odd harmonics in the ideal model. End corrections slightly increase the effective length of real pipes.',
r'f_n=\frac{nv}{2L}\ \text{(fixed string or open pipe)},\qquad f_n=\frac{(2n-1)v}{4L}\ \text{(one closed end)}',
'f_n mode frequency (Hz); n=1,2,… is mode index; v appropriate wave speed (m/s); L effective resonator length (m). Closed-pipe harmonic numbers are 1,3,5,… rather than n itself. Ideal boundary conditions are assumed.',
'The first overtone of a closed pipe is the third harmonic, not the second harmonic.',
'For a closed pipe L=0.85 m with sound speed 340 m/s, find fundamental and first overtone.',
'f₁=340/(4×0.85)=100 Hz. The next allowed mode is 3f₁=300 Hz.',
'An ideal closed pipe with fundamental 200 Hz has first overtone',
'300 Hz|400 Hz|600 Hz|800 Hz',2,
'Only odd harmonics occur, so the first overtone is 3×200=600 Hz.')
section(c,'beats','Beats and nearby frequencies',
'Two nearby frequencies produce an amplitude that grows and shrinks slowly. These loud–soft cycles are beats. Their rate equals the frequency difference, while the rapid oscillation is near the average frequency.',
'Beats help tune instruments: changing one frequency until the beats slow indicates the frequencies are getting closer. A beat rate alone gives the absolute difference and cannot tell which source is higher without another observation.',
r'f_b=|f_1-f_2|',
'f_b is beat frequency (beats/s or Hz); f₁ and f₂ are nearby source frequencies (Hz). Audible beats require suitable overlap and a small enough difference for the modulation to be perceived.',
'Five beats per second could mean a source is either 5 Hz above or 5 Hz below the reference.',
'A 256 Hz fork and another fork produce 4 beats/s. What frequencies are possible for the other fork?',
'It can be 252 or 260 Hz. A change that raises its frequency and reduces beats would identify 252 Hz as the starting value.',
'Sources at 300 and 306 Hz produce',
'3 beats/s|6 beats/s|303 beats/s|606 beats/s',1,
'The beat rate is the absolute difference 6 Hz, not the average or the sum.')
section(c,'doppler','Doppler effect for sound',
'A source moving toward an observer compresses the spacing of emitted wavefronts; an observer moving toward incoming waves encounters them faster. These are distinct effects because sound travels relative to the medium.',
'Choose signs by whether motion increases or decreases the observed frequency. For the simple collinear case below, both positive speed magnitudes describe approach. Recession reverses the appropriate sign. Wind requires velocities relative to the air.',
r"f'=f\frac{v+v_o}{v-v_s}\quad\text{(source and observer approaching)}",
'f emitted and f′ observed frequency (Hz); v sound speed relative to medium (m/s); v_o observer speed toward source and v_s source speed toward observer (m/s). Assume collinear subsonic motion and a stationary uniform medium.',
'A moving observer changes encounter rate; a moving source changes wavelength. Their denominators are therefore different.',
'A stationary observer hears a 500 Hz source approaching at 20 m/s in air with v=340 m/s. Find heard frequency.',
'f′=500×340/(340−20)=531.25 Hz. Approach raises the pitch.',
'A source receding from a stationary observer gives observed frequency',
'Higher than emitted|Lower than emitted|Always equal|Zero for every speed',1,
'Recession increases wavelength: f′=fv/(v+v_s), which is less than f for positive recession speed.')

c=chapter('thermal-properties','Thermal Properties of Matter',4,'105–281','Separate temperature from heat, then explain expansion, calorimetry, phase changes and heat transfer. Use absolute temperature whenever a law requires it.',10)
section(c,'temperature','Thermal equilibrium and temperature scales',
'Temperature tells whether bodies in thermal contact exchange energy as heat. Thermal equilibrium means no net heat transfer between them. The zeroth law makes a thermometer possible: bodies separately in equilibrium with a third are in equilibrium with one another.',
'Heat is energy in transfer because of temperature difference, not a substance stored in a body. Kelvin and Celsius have equal interval sizes but different zeros. Gas laws and radiation formulas require kelvin.',
r'T_K=t_C+273.15,\qquad \frac{t_F-32}{9}=\frac{t_C}{5}',
'T_K absolute temperature (K); t_C Celsius reading (°C); t_F Fahrenheit reading (°F). A 1 K temperature interval equals 1 °C; offsets apply to temperatures, not to temperature differences.',
'20°C is not twice the absolute temperature of 10°C.',
'Convert 27°C to kelvin and a rise of 10°C to kelvin units.',
'The temperature is 300.15 K (often 300 K in rounded problems). The temperature rise is 10 K, without adding 273.15.',
'A 25°C temperature increase equals',
'25 K|248.15 K|298.15 K|−25 K',0,
'Kelvin and Celsius intervals have the same size. The offset is used only when converting an actual temperature value.')
section(c,'expansion','Linear, area and volume expansion',
'Heating usually increases average separation between particles and expands a solid. Expansion coefficients compare fractional size change with temperature change. For an isotropic solid and small expansion, each direction expands similarly.',
'Area depends on two lengths and volume on three, producing β≈2α and γ≈3α to first order. A hole in a plate expands as if it were filled with the same material; heating does not make the surrounding material grow inward into it.',
r'\Delta L=\alpha L_0\Delta T,\quad \Delta A=\beta A_0\Delta T,\quad \Delta V=\gamma V_0\Delta T,\quad \beta\approx2\alpha,\quad\gamma\approx3\alpha',
'L₀/A₀/V₀ initial length (m), area (m²), volume (m³); α/β/γ linear/area/volume coefficients (K⁻¹); ΔT temperature change (K); Δ denotes change. Relations assume isotropy, approximately constant coefficients and small fractional changes.',
'Large temperature ranges can require temperature-dependent coefficients; the simple linear model is approximate.',
'A 2 m rod with α=12×10⁻⁶ K⁻¹ is heated by 50 K. Find length increase.',
'ΔL=12×10⁻⁶×2×50=0.0012 m=1.2 mm. The final length is 2.0012 m.',
'When a uniformly heated metal plate has a circular hole, the hole diameter generally',
'Decreases|Increases|Stays exactly fixed|Becomes zero',1,
'All distances in a freely expanding isotropic plate grow, including the hole diameter. A hypothetical plug of the same material would also expand.')
section(c,'liquids','Apparent expansion, density and anomalous water',
'A liquid in a vessel expands along with the vessel. The observed rise reflects the liquid’s volume expansion minus the vessel’s capacity expansion. Therefore apparent expansion is not the liquid’s true expansion coefficient.',
'Mass remains constant during ordinary thermal expansion, so density changes inversely with volume. Water behaves unusually between 0°C and 4°C: warming it contracts it, and its density is greatest near 4°C.',
r'\gamma_{\rm app}=\gamma_l-\gamma_v,\qquad \rho_T=\frac{\rho_0}{1+\gamma_l\Delta T}',
'γ_app apparent, γ_l liquid and γ_v vessel volumetric coefficients (K⁻¹); ρ₀ and ρ_T initial/final densities (kg/m³); ΔT temperature change (K). Assume no mass loss and constant coefficients over the interval; vessel coefficient is for capacity.',
'Use volume expansion for the vessel, not its linear coefficient without multiplying by three for an isotropic solid.',
'A liquid has γ_l=8×10⁻⁴ K⁻¹ and a vessel has γ_v=3×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹. Find apparent coefficient.',
'γ_app=0.0008−0.00003=7.7×10⁻⁴ K⁻¹. The vessel’s expansion hides part of the liquid’s true expansion.',
'Pure water warmed from 0°C to 4°C approximately',
'Expands and becomes less dense|Contracts and becomes denser|Keeps fixed density|Boils',1,
'This interval exhibits anomalous contraction. Maximum density is near 4°C, after which ordinary expansion dominates.')
section(c,'calorimetry','Specific heat and mixing',
'Specific heat capacity is energy needed per unit mass for a unit temperature rise without a phase change. Different materials respond differently to the same heat input. Heat capacity applies to the whole sample rather than per kilogram.',
'For an insulated mixture, energy lost by hotter parts equals energy gained by colder parts, including the container if its heat capacity matters. Check for a phase change before assuming that all heat simply changes temperature.',
r'Q=mc\Delta T=C\Delta T,\qquad \sum Q_i=0\quad\text{(isolated mixture)}',
'Q heat added (J); m mass (kg); c specific heat (J/kg K); C=mc sample heat capacity (J/K); ΔT signed temperature change (K). Sum includes all bodies and phase-change terms when needed; neglect heat loss only if stated.',
'An average of temperatures is valid only for equal heat capacities and no phase change.',
'Mix 0.2 kg of water at 80°C with 0.3 kg at 20°C in an insulated negligible-capacity container. Find final temperature.',
'Common specific heat cancels: 0.2(80−T)=0.3(T−20). Thus 16−0.2T=0.3T−6 and T=44°C.',
'Two samples of the same material have masses m and 2m. Their heat capacities are in ratio',
'1:1|1:2|2:1|1:4',1,
'C=mc, so doubling mass doubles sample heat capacity. Their specific heats remain equal.')
section(c,'latent','Latent heat and phase-change accounting',
'During a phase change at fixed pressure, energy can alter molecular arrangement without changing temperature. Specific latent heat is the energy per unit mass needed for the change. Melting and vaporisation have different latent heats.',
'In a mixed heating problem, calculate each stage separately: warm the initial phase, perform the phase change, then warm the new phase if energy remains. Insufficient heat may leave a two-phase mixture at the transition temperature.',
r'Q_{\rm phase}=mL,\qquad Q_{\rm total}=\sum mc\Delta T+\sum mL',
'm mass undergoing the stage (kg); L specific latent heat (J/kg); c stage-specific heat (J/kg K); Q energy (J). Phase-change signs are positive for melting/vaporisation and negative for freezing/condensation in heat-added accounting.',
'Heating a mixture of ice and water at 0°C first melts ice rather than immediately raising its temperature.',
'How much heat melts 0.10 kg of ice at 0°C, with L_f=3.34×10⁵ J/kg?',
'Q=0.10×3.34×10⁵=3.34×10⁴ J. This only melts the ice; warming the resulting water requires additional heat.',
'During melting at a fixed transition pressure, supplied heat mainly increases',
'Temperature|Phase-conversion energy|Mass|Cooling rate',1,
'Temperature remains at the melting point while the phase conversion proceeds. Energy changes internal arrangement rather than immediately raising temperature.')
section(c,'conduction','Conduction and thermal resistance',
'Conduction transfers energy through a material without bulk motion of the material. In a steady uniform slab, heat flows from the hot face to the cold face at a rate proportional to area and temperature difference and inversely proportional to thickness.',
'Thermal resistance makes composite slabs easier to analyse. Series layers carry the same heat rate and their temperature drops add, so their resistances add. Parallel paths share the temperature difference and their heat rates add.',
r'\dot Q=\frac{\kappa A(T_h-T_c)}{L},\qquad R_{th}=\frac{L}{\kappa A},\qquad R_{\rm series}=\sum R_i',
'Q̇ heat-transfer rate (W); κ conductivity (W/m K); A cross-sectional area (m²); L thickness (m); T_h/T_c face temperatures (K or consistent °C differences); R_th resistance (K/W). Assume steady one-dimensional conduction, constant κ and negligible side losses.',
'Conductivity is a material property; conductance κA/L also depends on geometry.',
'A slab has κ=0.5 W/m K, A=2 m², L=0.10 m and temperature difference 20 K. Find heat rate.',
'Q̇=0.5×2×20/0.10=200 W. Doubling the thickness halves this rate.',
'Two identical slabs placed in series have thermal resistance',
'Half one slab|Equal to one slab|Twice one slab|Four times one slab',2,
'The same heat rate crosses both and the temperature drops add, so the combined resistance is 2R.')
section(c,'convection','Convection and thermal radiation',
'Convection transfers heat through moving fluid: heated fluid often becomes less dense and rises under gravity, while cooler fluid replaces it. Radiation transfers energy electromagnetically and can operate through vacuum.',
'All bodies emit thermal radiation; a cooler environment also radiates toward the body. Net radiative loss subtracts the incoming contribution. A black body is an ideal perfect absorber and emitter, while emissivity describes departures from that ideal.',
r'P_{\rm net}=\varepsilon\sigma A(T^4-T_s^4),\qquad \lambda_{\max}T=b',
'P_net outward radiative power (W); ε emissivity (0 to 1); σ Stefan–Boltzmann constant (W/m² K⁴); A radiating area (m²); T body and T_s surrounding absolute temperatures (K); λ_max peak wavelength (m); b Wien constant (m K). The net formula assumes large uniform surroundings and applicable grey-body emissivity.',
'Fourth-power radiation and Wien’s law require kelvin, never Celsius.',
'An ideal black body’s temperature doubles. How do emitted power per area and peak wavelength change?',
'Emitted power scales as T⁴, increasing sixteenfold. Peak wavelength scales as 1/T, halving. Net power must also account for surroundings.',
'Heat can cross empty space by',
'Conduction alone|Convection alone|Thermal radiation|Bulk fluid motion',2,
'Radiation needs no material medium. Conduction and convection require matter.')
section(c,'cooling','Newton’s cooling law and measurement',
'For a small temperature excess under approximately constant heat-transfer conditions, the heat-loss rate is proportional to the excess above the surroundings. As the object cools, the excess decreases and cooling slows.',
'Combine the proportional loss rate with the sample’s heat capacity to obtain an exponential decay of temperature excess. The approximation can come from convection or a small-excess expansion of radiation, but is unreliable for very large temperature differences or changing surroundings.',
r'\frac{dT}{dt}=-\lambda(T-T_s),\qquad T-T_s=(T_0-T_s)e^{-\lambda t}',
'T/T₀ current/initial temperature and T_s constant surroundings temperature (K or °C with differences); t time (s); λ positive cooling constant (s⁻¹) incorporating heat transfer and heat capacity. Assumes small excess and constant conditions.',
'Equal successive time intervals produce equal fractional decreases in excess temperature, not equal temperature drops.',
'An object’s excess temperature halves every 5 minutes. Starting 40°C above surroundings, what excess remains after 10 minutes?',
'Two halving intervals leave 40×(1/2)²=10°C above surroundings. Add the surroundings temperature only if actual temperature is requested.',
'Under Newton cooling, as a body approaches room temperature its cooling rate magnitude',
'Increases|Decreases|Stays nonzero and constant|Changes sign every second',1,
'The rate is proportional to the temperature excess, which shrinks toward zero.')

c=chapter('kinetic-theory','Kinetic Theory of Gases',4,'282–325','Connect random molecular motion to pressure and temperature. Keep molecular mass, molar mass and the number of particles distinct.',12)
section(c,'gas-law','Ideal-gas model and equation of state',
'An ideal gas models molecules as negligibly small particles with negligible interactions except elastic collisions. It approximates dilute gases away from condensation. Temperature must be absolute and pressure must be absolute rather than gauge.',
'The mole form counts amount of substance, while the particle form counts molecules. Boyle’s, Charles’s and pressure–temperature laws follow by holding the appropriate variable fixed. Real gases can depart from this model at high density or low temperature.',
r'pV=nRT=Nk_BT,\qquad N=nN_A,\qquad R=N_Ak_B',
'p pressure (Pa); V volume (m³); n amount (mol); R gas constant (J/mol K); N molecule count; k_B Boltzmann constant (J/K); T absolute temperature (K); N_A Avogadro constant (mol⁻¹). Ideal thermal equilibrium is assumed.',
'Use the same particle definition when counting N; dissociating molecules changes the number of particles.',
'At fixed amount and volume, an ideal gas warms from 300 to 450 K. Find the pressure ratio.',
'p∝T under these constraints, so p₂/p₁=450/300=1.5.',
'Boyle’s law pV=constant requires constant',
'Temperature and gas amount|Pressure only|Volume only|Temperature in Celsius numerically',0,
'For a fixed gas amount, pV=nRT stays fixed at constant absolute temperature. It is an isothermal relation.')
section(c,'pressure','Pressure from molecular momentum transfer',
'Gas pressure comes from molecules changing momentum when they collide with container walls. In equilibrium their random directions are isotropic, so the average squared velocity component in each direction is one third of the total.',
'Bulk motion of the whole container does not add to pressure: collisions depend on velocities relative to the walls. Derivation assumes elastic wall impacts and many molecules, giving a smooth time-averaged force.',
r'p=\frac13\rho\langle v^2\rangle=\frac13\rho v_{rms}^2',
'p pressure (Pa); ρ gas mass density (kg/m³); ⟨v²⟩ mean squared molecular speed relative to the gas bulk (m²/s²); v_rms its square root (m/s). Assume equilibrium isotropic random motion and ideal elastic collisions.',
'Mean vector velocity can be zero while mean squared speed is positive.',
'A gas has density 1.2 kg/m³ and rms speed 500 m/s. Find pressure.',
'p=(1/3)×1.2×500²=100000 Pa. The molecular speeds are random relative to the gas, not a common drift velocity.',
'A sealed equilibrium gas container moving at constant velocity has pressure determined by',
'Its ground-frame speed alone|Random molecular motion relative to the walls|The sum of all molecular velocities|The sign of its height',1,
'A uniform translation adds the same velocity to walls and molecules. Relative collision speeds, temperature and pressure are unchanged.')
section(c,'speeds','Molecular energy and characteristic speeds',
'Absolute temperature measures average translational kinetic energy per molecule in an ideal gas. Equal temperatures give equal mean translational energies for different species, but lighter molecules need higher speeds to carry that energy.',
'Rms speed, mean speed and most probable speed are different summaries of the speed distribution. For an equilibrium ideal gas they follow a fixed ordering. Use molecular mass with k_B or molar mass with R, without mixing the two.',
r'\langle K_{tr}\rangle=\tfrac32k_BT,\quad v_{rms}=\sqrt{\frac{3RT}{M}},\quad \bar v=\sqrt{\frac{8RT}{\pi M}},\quad v_{mp}=\sqrt{\frac{2RT}{M}}',
'K_tr mean translational energy per molecule (J); k_B constant (J/K); T temperature (K); R gas constant; M molar mass (kg/mol); v_rms/mean/most-probable speeds in m/s. Assume Maxwell equilibrium distribution.',
'Molar mass in g/mol must be converted to kg/mol when using SI R.',
'At the same temperature, compare rms speeds of gases with molar masses 4 and 16 g/mol.',
'v₄/v₁₆=√(16/4)=2. The lighter species is twice as fast, while both have the same average translational energy per molecule.',
'For an ideal equilibrium gas, the characteristic speed ordering is',
'v_mp > v_rms > mean|v_rms > mean > v_mp|All equal|Mean > v_rms > v_mp',1,
'The squared-speed weighting raises rms above the ordinary mean. The most probable speed is √(2RT/M), the smallest of the three.')
section(c,'equipartition','Degrees of freedom and heat capacities',
'Each active quadratic degree of freedom contributes k_BT/2 per molecule on average in the classical model. A monatomic gas has three translational degrees; a diatomic gas at ordinary temperatures often has three translational and two rotational ones.',
'Vibrational modes can become active at higher temperatures and are suppressed at lower temperatures by quantum effects. Heat capacities therefore depend on which modes are active; do not apply a single diatomic value at every temperature.',
r'U=\tfrac f2nRT,\quad C_V=\tfrac f2R,\quad C_P=C_V+R,\quad \gamma=\frac{C_P}{C_V}=\frac{f+2}{f}',
'U internal energy (J); f number of active quadratic degrees of freedom per molecule; n amount (mol); T temperature (K); C_V/C_P molar capacities (J/mol K); γ ratio. Ideal gas with temperature-independent f over the interval is assumed.',
'Each harmonic vibrational mode adds two quadratic contributions, one kinetic and one potential.',
'For a monatomic ideal gas, find C_V, C_P and γ.',
'f=3 gives C_V=3R/2, C_P=5R/2 and γ=5/3. Internal energy includes translational kinetic energy in this ideal model.',
'A diatomic ideal gas with rotations active and vibrations inactive has γ',
'1|7/5|5/3|2',1,
'f=5, so γ=(f+2)/f=7/5. The monatomic value 5/3 uses only three degrees of freedom.')
section(c,'mixtures','Gas mixtures and partial pressure',
'For an ideal nonreacting mixture, each species contributes a partial pressure according to its molecule count at the common temperature and volume. The total pressure is the sum of these partial pressures.',
'The common temperature means equal average translational energy per molecule, not equal rms speeds. If a chemical process changes molecule count, recompute total amount before comparing pressures.',
r'p_{\rm total}=\sum p_i,\qquad p_i=\frac{n_iRT}{V},\qquad \frac{p_i}{p_{\rm total}}=\frac{n_i}{\sum n_j}',
'p_i partial pressure (Pa); n_i species amount (mol); R gas constant; T common temperature (K); V shared volume (m³); i/j species indices. Dalton’s law assumes an ideal nonreacting mixture at equilibrium.',
'Mole fraction, not mass fraction, directly sets ideal partial-pressure fraction.',
'A mixture contains 1 mol of one gas and 3 mol of another at total pressure 8×10⁴ Pa. Find their partial pressures.',
'Mole fractions are 1/4 and 3/4. Pressures are 2×10⁴ and 6×10⁴ Pa, respectively.',
'If molecular dissociation doubles total particle count at unchanged T and V, pressure',
'Halves|Doubles|Stays fixed|Quadruples',1,
'pV=Nk_BT, so doubling N at fixed T and V doubles p. Conserved mass does not mean conserved molecule count.')
section(c,'mean-free-path','Mean free path and collisions',
'Molecules travel between collisions along approximately straight segments. Mean free path is the average distance between collisions, not the average distance between neighbouring molecules. Higher number density makes collisions more frequent.',
'For the hard-sphere model the collision cross section depends on diameter squared. The √2 factor accounts for relative molecular motion. Combining number density with the ideal gas law shows that at fixed pressure the path grows with temperature.',
r'\ell_{mfp}=\frac1{\sqrt2\pi d^2 n_N}=\frac{k_BT}{\sqrt2\pi d^2p}',
'ℓ_mfp mean free path (m); d effective molecular diameter (m); n_N=N/V number density (m⁻³), not moles; k_B constant; T temperature (K); p pressure (Pa). Dilute identical hard spheres and an equilibrium ideal gas are assumed.',
'At fixed volume and particle count, raising temperature does not change this hard-sphere mean free path, although collision frequency changes.',
'At fixed temperature and molecular diameter, pressure triples. What happens to mean free path?',
'ℓ_mfp is inversely proportional to p, so it becomes one third. Number density has tripled.',
'If effective molecular diameter doubles at fixed number density, mean free path becomes',
'Half|One quarter|Twice|Four times',1,
'The denominator contains d²; doubling d quadruples the collision cross section and reduces the path to one quarter.')

c=chapter('thermodynamics','Thermodynamics',4,'326–398','Account for heat, work and internal energy with one consistent sign convention. Explain gas processes before applying their equations, then study cycles and engines.',11)
section(c,'first-law','State variables and the first law',
'A thermodynamic state is specified by equilibrium variables such as pressure, volume and temperature. Internal energy is a state function: its change depends only on the endpoints. Heat and work describe transfers along a process and depend on its path.',
'We take heat Q positive into the gas and work W positive when done by the gas. Energy conservation then says that input heat increases internal energy or supplies outward work. Compression gives negative gas work under this convention.',
r'\Delta U=Q-W,\qquad \Delta U=nC_V\Delta T\quad\text{(ideal gas, constant }C_V)',
'ΔU internal-energy change (J); Q heat added (J); W work done by gas (J); n gas amount (mol); C_V molar constant-volume heat capacity (J/mol K); ΔT temperature change (K). Ideal-gas U depends only on T; capacities can vary with temperature.',
'Do not mix the work-by-gas sign convention with a formula written for work-on-gas.',
'A gas receives 500 J heat and does 200 J work. Find internal-energy change.',
'ΔU=500−200=300 J. The gas retains part of the supplied energy and transfers the rest as work.',
'A gas is compressed adiabatically with 100 J of work done on it. Its ΔU is',
'−100 J|0|+100 J|+200 J',2,
'Q=0 and work by gas W=−100 J. Thus ΔU=0−(−100)=+100 J.')
section(c,'work','Pressure–volume work and paths',
'A gas does work when it moves a piston against external pressure. For a quasistatic reversible process, the gas pressure nearly matches the external pressure and the work is the area under the gas p–V curve.',
'Expansion gives positive work, compression negative work. Two processes connecting the same endpoints can enclose different areas and therefore have different W and Q while sharing the same ΔU. In a general irreversible process, use external resisting pressure for boundary work.',
r'W=\int_{V_i}^{V_f}p_{\rm ext}\,dV,\quad W=p(V_f-V_i)\quad\text{(constant pressure)}',
'W work by gas (J); p_ext external resisting pressure (Pa); V_i/V_f initial/final volumes (m³); p constant pressure. A reversible quasistatic path permits p_ext≈p_gas; gas p–V area is not automatically valid for arbitrary irreversible paths.',
'A litre is 10⁻³ m³; pressure–volume area then has joule units.',
'A gas expands from 2 to 5 litres against constant 10⁵ Pa. Find work.',
'ΔV=3×10⁻³ m³, so W=10⁵×3×10⁻³=300 J.',
'A gas held at fixed volume does boundary expansion work',
'Equal to Q|Equal to ΔU|Zero|Equal to pV',2,
'dV=0 throughout, so ∫p_ext dV=0. Heat can still change internal energy and pressure.')
section(c,'isochoric-isobaric','Isochoric and isobaric heating',
'In an isochoric process volume stays fixed, so no expansion work is done and all added heat changes internal energy. In an isobaric process, part of the heat also provides expansion work, giving a larger heat requirement for the same ideal-gas temperature rise.',
'For an ideal gas at constant pressure, pΔV=nRΔT. Applying the first law gives the molar heat-capacity relation C_P−C_V=R. This relation is specific to an ideal gas; it is not a universal identity for every material.',
r'Q_V=nC_V\Delta T,\quad Q_P=nC_P\Delta T,\quad W_P=nR\Delta T,\quad C_P-C_V=R',
'Q_V/Q_P heat added at fixed volume/pressure (J); n amount (mol); C_V/C_P molar capacities (J/mol K); ΔT temperature rise (K); W_P gas work (J); R gas constant. Assume ideal gas and constant capacities over the interval.',
'Isochoric means fixed volume; it does not mean fixed temperature or zero heat.',
'One mole of monatomic ideal gas warms by 20 K at fixed pressure. Find Q, W and ΔU in terms of R.',
'C_P=5R/2 and C_V=3R/2. Thus Q=50R J, W=20R J and ΔU=30R J; Q−W=ΔU.',
'For the same ideal-gas temperature rise, heat required at fixed pressure is',
'Less than at fixed volume|More than at fixed volume|Always zero|Always the same',1,
'C_P=C_V+R, so fixed-pressure heating requires additional heat to supply expansion work.')
section(c,'isothermal','Isothermal processes and free expansion',
'An ideal gas undergoing an isothermal process has unchanged internal energy because temperature is fixed. Heat and work can still be nonzero. For reversible expansion, heat from a reservoir replaces the energy transferred as work.',
'Free expansion into vacuum is different: the external pressure is zero, so boundary work is zero. If also insulated, Q=0 and ΔU=0. An ideal gas then has the same final temperature, but the irreversible process must not be assigned the reversible logarithmic work.',
r'W_{\rm rev}=nRT\ln\frac{V_f}{V_i},\quad Q=W_{\rm rev},\quad \Delta U=0\quad\text{(ideal isothermal)}',
'W_rev reversible isothermal work (J); n amount (mol); R gas constant; T fixed absolute temperature (K); V_i/V_f volumes (m³); ln is natural logarithm. Its argument is dimensionless. Q=W applies to the ideal isothermal energy balance.',
'Constant temperature does not mean no heat transfer; insulation means no heat transfer.',
'One mole expands reversibly at 300 K to twice its volume. Find work using R=8.31.',
'W=1×8.31×300×ln2≈1728 J. ΔU=0, so the same heat must enter from the reservoir.',
'An insulated ideal gas expands freely into vacuum. Work by the gas is',
'nRT ln(V_f/V_i)|Zero|p_iV_i|Always negative',1,
'The external resisting pressure is zero, so boundary work vanishes. The reversible logarithmic formula describes a different path.')
section(c,'adiabatic','Adiabatic change and connected-cylinder reasoning',
'Adiabatic means no heat crosses the boundary. A rapid expansion or good insulation can approximately achieve it. Expansion work then comes from internal energy and lowers the temperature of an ideal gas; compression raises it.',
'The pV^γ relation requires a reversible quasistatic adiabatic ideal-gas process. In connected rigid insulated cylinders, choose both gases as the system: no external boundary work and no external heat imply total internal-energy conservation. A valve opening is generally irreversible, so do not apply pV^γ to each side during mixing.',
r'pV^\gamma=\text{constant},\quad TV^{\gamma-1}=\text{constant},\quad W=\frac{p_iV_i-p_fV_f}{\gamma-1},\quad U_{1i}+U_{2i}=U_{1f}+U_{2f}\ \text{(isolated rigid pair)}',
'p pressure (Pa), V volume (m³), T temperature (K), γ=C_P/C_V dimensionless; W work by gas (J), U gas internal energies (J); i/f initial/final. First three require a reversible adiabatic ideal gas with constant γ. The last requires a rigid insulated combined system without other energy exchanges.',
'A connecting valve does not by itself make a process reversible or isothermal. Specify insulation, wall motion and gas species.',
'Two rigid insulated vessels contain the same ideal gas: 1 mol at 300 K and 1 mol at 500 K. After connecting and equilibration, find final temperature.',
'For equal constant C_V, total energy conservation gives C_V(300+500)=2C_V T_f, hence T_f=400 K. Final common pressure then uses the total volume in pV=nRT.',
'In a reversible adiabatic ideal-gas expansion, temperature',
'Increases|Decreases|Must remain fixed|Becomes zero immediately',1,
'Q=0 and W>0 imply ΔU<0. Since ideal-gas U depends on T, temperature falls.')
section(c,'cycles','Cycles, heat engines and efficiency',
'A thermodynamic cycle returns to its initial state, so the net internal-energy change is zero. Net heat input equals net work output. On a p–V plot a clockwise loop represents positive net work by the gas.',
'A heat engine absorbs energy from a hot source, delivers some as work, and rejects the remainder to a colder sink. Efficiency uses work divided by heat absorbed from the hot source, not divided by the net heat input.',
r'\Delta U_{\rm cycle}=0,\quad W=Q_h-Q_c,\quad \eta=\frac{W}{Q_h}=1-\frac{Q_c}{Q_h}',
'W net cycle work (J); Q_h positive heat absorbed from hot source (J); Q_c positive magnitude rejected to cold sink (J); η dimensionless efficiency. The engine returns to its starting state after each cycle.',
'Q_c is defined here as a positive magnitude; heat-added signs in a process equation must still be handled consistently.',
'An engine takes 800 J from a source and rejects 500 J per cycle. Find work and efficiency.',
'W=800−500=300 J. Efficiency is 300/800=0.375=37.5%.',
'A gas completing a cycle has net internal-energy change',
'Equal to net heat|Equal to work|Zero|Equal to heat rejected',2,
'Internal energy is a state function. Returning to the same state makes its change zero, even though heat and work around the cycle can be nonzero.')
section(c,'second-law','Second law, Carnot limits and refrigerators',
'The first law conserves energy but does not decide which processes occur spontaneously. The second law rules out converting heat from a single reservoir entirely into cyclic work and requires external work to pump heat from cold to hot.',
'A reversible Carnot engine sets the maximum efficiency between two temperatures. Real engines have irreversible losses. A refrigerator’s coefficient of performance compares heat removed from the cold region with supplied work; it can exceed one because it measures moved heat, not an energy-conversion fraction.',
r'\eta_C=1-\frac{T_c}{T_h},\qquad COP=\frac{Q_c}{W},\qquad COP_C=\frac{T_c}{T_h-T_c}',
'T_h/T_c hot/cold reservoir temperatures (K), T_h>T_c>0; η_C maximum reversible engine efficiency; Q_c heat removed from cold reservoir (J); W input work (J); COP coefficient of performance; COP_C reversible refrigerator limit. Kelvin is essential.',
'An efficiency fraction cannot exceed one; refrigerator COP can because work helps move additional heat.',
'A Carnot engine operates between 600 and 300 K. Find maximum efficiency. A reversible refrigerator between the same temperatures has what COP?',
'η_C=1−300/600=0.5=50%. Refrigerator COP_C=300/(600−300)=1.',
'A Carnot engine between 500 and 300 K has maximum efficiency',
'20%|40%|60%|80%',1,
'η=1−300/500=0.4. Using Celsius instead of absolute reservoir temperatures would give an incorrect ratio.')

# Additional modules complete relationships that are often tested across chapters.
c=next(c for c in CHAPTERS if c['key']=='maths')
section(c,'logs','Exponentials and logarithms',
'An exponential describes a fixed fractional change per equal interval, such as cooling toward room temperature. A logarithm answers the inverse question: what exponent gives a particular ratio? Its argument must be dimensionless.',
'Natural logarithms use base e and appear in integration of 1/x and in reversible isothermal gas work. Logarithms turn products into sums; logarithms of a ratio turn into differences. These operations apply only to positive real arguments in the elementary formulas here.',
r'\ln(ab)=\ln a+\ln b,\quad \ln(a/b)=\ln a-\ln b,\quad \ln(e^x)=x',
'a and b are positive dimensionless numbers; x is a dimensionless exponent; ln is the natural logarithm. The argument of an exponential must also be dimensionless, for example a cooling constant times time.',
'ln(V_f/V_i) is valid; ln of a dimensional volume alone is not a complete physical expression.',
'For a temperature excess θ=θ₀e^(−λt), when does the excess halve?',
'½=e^(−λt), so taking ln gives −ln2=−λt and t=ln2/λ. λ must have inverse-time units.',
'ln(e³) equals',
'1|3|e|9',1,
'Natural logarithm is the inverse of the exponential, so it returns the exponent 3.')
c=next(c for c in CHAPTERS if c['key']=='work')
section(c,'elastic-results','One-dimensional elastic collision results',
'Elastic head-on collisions conserve both momentum and kinetic energy. Solving the two equations gives final velocities or, equivalently, conservation of momentum plus reversal of relative velocity.',
'The unequal-mass formulas reveal useful limits: a light projectile reflecting from a very heavy stationary target nearly reverses its velocity; equal masses exchange velocities. Keep signed velocities when both objects initially move.',
r'v_1=\frac{(m_1-m_2)u_1+2m_2u_2}{m_1+m_2},\quad v_2=\frac{2m_1u_1+(m_2-m_1)u_2}{m_1+m_2}',
'm₁/m₂ masses (kg); u₁/u₂ initial and v₁/v₂ final signed velocities (m/s). Assumptions: one-dimensional perfectly elastic collision and negligible external impulse; denominator is positive total mass.',
'These formulas are not valid for sticking or energy-losing collisions.',
'A 1 kg cart at 6 m/s hits a stationary 2 kg cart elastically. Find both velocities.',
'v₁=(1−2)6/3=−2 m/s and v₂=2×1×6/3=4 m/s. Momentum is −2+8=6 kg m/s; energy is 2+16=18 J, matching the initial values.',
'A very light ball reflects elastically from a stationary effectively immovable wall. Its final velocity is approximately',
'u|−u|0|u/2',1,
'The wall-mass limit gives velocity reversal with the same speed. Its momentum changes even though its kinetic energy does not.')
c=next(c for c in CHAPTERS if c['key']=='gravitation')
section(c,'spherical','Shells, solid spheres and rotation of Earth',
'Spherical symmetry makes the shell theorem possible. A uniform thin shell has zero field inside and acts as a central point mass outside. A uniform solid sphere instead has an interior field proportional to radius because enclosed mass grows as r³.',
'Earth’s rotation reduces apparent weight because some gravitational acceleration supplies the required centripetal acceleration. This reduction is largest at the equator and zero at the poles in a spherical model. Actual latitude dependence also includes Earth’s shape.',
r'g_{\rm inside}=\frac{GMr}{R^3},\quad V_{\rm inside}=-\frac{GM}{2R}\left(3-\frac{r^2}{R^2}\right),\quad g_{\rm eq,app}=g_0-\Omega^2R',
'G gravitational constant; M sphere mass (kg); R radius and r interior centre distance (m), 0≤r≤R; g field magnitude (m/s²); V potential (J/kg), zero at infinity. Interior formulas require uniform density. Ω Earth angular speed (rad/s); g₀ gravitational surface value; equatorial apparent value uses a spherical rotating Earth.',
'At a sphere’s centre, field is zero but potential is its most negative value.',
'Find the potential at the centre of a uniform sphere compared with its surface potential.',
'At r=0, V=−3GM/(2R). At the surface V=−GM/R. The centre potential is 1.5 times as negative, despite zero field there.',
'Rotation alone makes apparent surface gravity smallest at the',
'North pole|South pole|Equator|Same at all latitudes',2,
'Equatorial points require the greatest centripetal acceleration for Earth’s rotation, leaving a smaller normal force per mass.')
c=next(c for c in CHAPTERS if c['key']=='oscillations')
section(c,'superposition','Combining SHMs and an oscillating liquid column',
'Two same-frequency SHMs along one line combine into another SHM with an amplitude determined by phase, not simply by adding amplitude magnitudes. Equal perpendicular SHMs a quarter-cycle apart trace a circle.',
'A displaced liquid column in a uniform U-tube has a restoring pressure difference because one side rises while the other falls. For a total liquid length L and a displacement x on one side, the height difference is 2x. Newton’s law then gives SHM with a period independent of density.',
r'x=a\sin\omega t+b\cos\omega t,\quad A=\sqrt{a^2+b^2},\qquad T_{U}=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L}{2g}}',
'a/b coefficients and A resultant amplitude (m); ω angular frequency (rad/s); t time (s); T_U U-tube period (s); L total moving liquid-column length (m); g gravity. U-tube assumes uniform area, incompressible liquid, small motions and negligible viscosity.',
'The tube period uses the total moving liquid length, not just the initial level difference.',
'For x=3 sinωt+4 cosωt cm, find resultant amplitude.',
'The sine and cosine components differ in phase by π/2. Their resultant amplitude is √(3²+4²)=5 cm.',
'For ideal U-tube oscillations with fixed liquid length, doubling density changes period by',
'A factor of 2|A factor of √2|No change|A factor of 1/2',2,
'Both restoring force and moving mass scale with density; it cancels, leaving T=2π√(L/2g).')
# Attach useful qualitative bridges beside the relevant introduced concept.
s=next(c for c in CHAPTERS if c['key']=='thermal-properties')['sections'][1]
s['reasoning'] += ' A bonded bimetallic strip bends because its layers want different lengths: the more-expanding layer becomes the longer outer, convex side.'
s=next(c for c in CHAPTERS if c['key']=='waves')['sections'][0]
s['reasoning'] += ' At a stationary boundary between media the source frequency remains the same; changed propagation speed changes wavelength according to λ=v_w/f.'
